(window.LH_SEARCH = window.LH_SEARCH || {})["testng"] = {"s":"testng","t":[["tng-basics","TestNG Foundations: Annotations, Assertions and Test Control","1.1"],["tng-suites","Suites, Groups, Parameters and Data Providers","1.2"],["tng-advanced","Listeners, Retries, Factories and Framework Design","1.3"],["cheatsheet","Cheatsheet","📄"],["interview-questions","Interview Questions","🎤"]],"c":[[0,"","TestNG Foundations: Annotations, Assertions and Test Control","What TestNG adds to plain Java, setting it up with Maven, writing a first test class, the full annotation lifecycle from @BeforeSuite to @AfterSuite, hard and soft assertions, test attributes such as priority, enabled, description, timeOut, invocationCount and expectedExceptions, the default run order, and dependencies with dependsOnMethods and alwaysRun. · TestNG · test framework · Maven dependency · pom.xml · @Test · @BeforeSuite · @AfterSuite · @BeforeTest · @AfterTest · @BeforeClass · @AfterClass · @BeforeMethod · @AfterMethod · annotations · lifecycle · Assert · assertEquals · assertTrue · assertFalse · assertNotNull · SoftAssert · assertAll · hard assert · soft assert · priority · enabled · description · timeOut · invocationCount · expectedExceptions · expectedExceptionsMessageRegExp · dependsOnMethods · alwaysRun · skipped tests · run order · pytest · fixtures · Mocha · hooks ✅ Brainy Bytes · TestNG ✅ TestNG›🧪 TestNG Framework›TestNG foundations 🧪 Step 1 · Topic 1 of 3 · Beginner to advanced"],[0,"top","","A main method can run checks, but it cannot report which ones passed, skip the rest when setup fails, or rerun only the failures. TestNG does all of that through annotations. Every example on this page runs on real TestNG, and the same idea is shown in pytest and Mocha, so you can compare the three most common test runners for testers. ⏱️ About 8 min read📚 6 parts🎬 2 animations🏋️ 8 practice problems🎯 16 quiz questions🧮 8 calculationsJava with real TestNG, with pytest (Python) and Mocha (JavaScript) side by sideThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Why a test framework 2A first test class 3The annotation lifecycle 4Hard and soft assertions 5Test attributes 6Dependencies between tests Lesson 1 of 6"],[0,"why-testng","Why a test framework","Need Plain main method TestNG Know which checks passed Read the printed lines yourself Pass, fail and skip counts, HTML and XML reports Keep going after one failure The first exception stops everything Each @Test is independent Set up and clean up Copy the code into every check @BeforeMethod, @AfterClass and the rest Choose what to run Comment code in and out testng.xml, groups, include and exclude Run with different data Loops written by hand @DataProvider and @Parameters Rerun only failures Not possible testng-failed.xml, retry analyzers <!-- pom.xml --> <dependency> <groupId>org.testng</groupId> <artifactId>testng</artifactId> <version>7.10.2</version> <scope>test</scope> </dependency> Test classes go in src/test/java. Run them from the IDE (right click, Run), from a testng.xml file, or with mvn test, where the Maven Surefire plugin finds the tests. After a run, TestNG writes its reports to test-output (or target/surefire-reports with Maven), including emailable-report.html and testng-failed.xml. The examples here run on TestNG 6.9, which is what this site's code runner has. Everything shown works the same in TestNG 7, the version to use in new projects. In pytest the same roles are played by test functions and fixtures, in Mocha by describe, it and hooks. Lesson 2 of 6"],[0,"first-test","A first test class","A test method is a public void method marked @Test. It calls the code under test and checks the result with an assertion. If every assertion holds, the test passes. The first assertion that fails throws an AssertionError, and TestNG records the test as failed and moves on to the next one. TestNG's Assert.assertEquals(actual, expected) takes the actual value first. JUnit uses the opposite order. Swapping them still passes and fails correctly, but the failure message then says \"expected 3 but found 4\", which sends you looking in the wrong place. Four tests on a Calculator, one with a wrong expectation classCalculator: # the code under testdef add(self, a, b): return a + b def divide(self, a, b): return a // b def is_even(self, n): return n % 2 == 0 calc = Calculator() def test_adds_two_numbers(): assert calc.add(2, 3) == 5def test_adds_two_negatives(): assert calc.add(-2, -3) == -5, \"sum of two negatives\"def test_detects_even_and_odd(): assert calc.is_even(10) assertnot calc.is_even(7) def test_divides_seven_by_two(): # this test exposes a wrong expectationassert calc.divide(7, 2) == 4, \"7 / 2 in int arithmetic\" Real output of the Python version ============================= test session starts ============================== collecting ... collected 4 items test_prog.py::test_adds_two_numbers PASSED [ 25%] test_prog.py::test_adds_two_negatives PASSED [ 50%] test_prog.py::test_detects_even_and_odd PASSED [ 75%] test_prog.py::test_divides_seven_by_two FAILED [100%] =================================== FAILURES =================================== __________________________ test_divides_seven_by_two ___________________________ def test_divides_seven_by_two(): # this test exposes a wrong expectation > assert calc.divide(7, 2) == 4, \"7 / 2 in int arithmetic\" E AssertionError: 7 / 2 in int arithmetic E assert 3 == 4 E + where 3 = divide(7, 2) E + where divide = <test_prog.Calculator object>.divide test_prog.py:24: AssertionError =========================== short test summary info ============================ FAILED test_prog.py::test_divides_seven_by_two - AssertionError: 7 / 2 in int... ========================= 1 failed, 3 passed in 0.05s ========================== Lesson 3 of 6"],[0,"lifecycle","The annotation lifecycle","Annotation Runs Typical use in UI automation @BeforeSuite / @AfterSuite Once for the whole run Start and close the report, load configuration @BeforeTest / @AfterTest Once per <test> tag in testng.xml Set the browser for that block of tests @BeforeClass / @AfterClass Once per test class Launch and quit the browser @BeforeMethod / @AfterMethod Before and after every @Test Open the start page, clear cookies, take a screenshot on failure 🎬 Try it"],[0,"lifecycle","Build a suite and watch the call order","Choose how many <test> tags, classes and test methods the suite has, then play. Each line is one method TestNG calls, indented by level. The counters show how often each annotation ran. <test> tags 1Classes per test 2Tests per class 2 @BeforeSuitestart the report @BeforeTest<test name=\"Chrome\"> @BeforeClassLoginTests @BeforeMethodfresh page @TestLoginTests.validLogin @AfterMethodclean up @BeforeMethodfresh page @TestLoginTests.wrongPassword @AfterMethodclean up @AfterClassLoginTests @BeforeClassCartTests @BeforeMethodfresh page @TestCartTests.addItem @AfterMethodclean up @BeforeMethodfresh page @TestCartTests.removeItem @AfterMethodclean up @AfterClassCartTests @AfterTest</test> @AfterSuitesend the report @BeforeSuite1 @BeforeTest0 @BeforeClass0 @BeforeMethod0 @Test0 @AfterMethod0 @AfterClass0 @AfterTest0 @AfterSuite0 Now: @BeforeSuite. With 1 <test> tag, 2 classes each and 2 tests per class, TestNG runs 4 tests and calls @BeforeMethod 4 times, @BeforeClass 2 times, @BeforeTest 1 time and @BeforeSuite once. 0 / 19 Every annotation printing when it runs import pytest # pytest fixtures play the role of TestNG's @Before/@After annotations.# The code before yield is the setup, the code after yield is the teardown.@pytest.fixture(scope=\"session\", autouse=True) def suite(): print(\"\\n@BeforeSuite once, before everything (start the report)\") yieldprint(\"@AfterSuite once, after everything (send the report)\") @pytest.fixture(scope=\"module\", autouse=True) def browser(): print(\" @BeforeClass once for this module (launch the browser)\") yieldprint(\" @AfterClass once for this module (quit the browser)\") @pytest.fixture(autouse=True) def page(): print(\" @BeforeMethod before every test (open the login page)\") yieldprint(\" @AfterMethod after every test (clear cookies)\") def test_login_with_valid_user(): print(\" test login_with_valid_user\") def test_logout(): print(\" test logout\") Real output of the Python version ============================= test session starts ============================== collecting ... collected 2 items test_prog.py::test_login_with_valid_user @BeforeSuite once, before everything (start the report) @BeforeClass once for this module (launch the browser) @BeforeMethod before every test (open the login page) test login_with_valid_user PASSED @AfterMethod after every test (clear cookies) test_prog.py::test_logout @BeforeMethod before every test (open the login page) test logout PASSED @AfterMethod after every test (clear cookies) @AfterClass once for this module (quit the browser) @AfterSuite once, after everything (send the report) ============================== 2 passed in 0.05s =============================== pytest builds the same layers with fixture scopes: session, module, class and function. The code before yield is the setup, the code after it the teardown. Mocha has before, beforeEach, afterEach and after. Lesson 4 of 6"],[0,"assertions","Hard and soft assertions","Hard assert (Assert) Stops the test at the first failure. Use it when the remaining steps make no sense after a failure, for example when login itself failed. Soft assert (SoftAssert) Records each failure and keeps going. assertAll() at the end fails the test with every problem listed. Use it to check many fields on one page. Forgetting soft.assertAll() is a classic bug: every check fails silently and the test still passes. Create a new SoftAssert inside each test, never share one between tests. The same page checked with hard and soft assertions title, cart_count, user = \"Shop | Home\", 2, \"guest\"classSoftAssert: \"\"\"pytest has no soft assert built in (the pytest-check plugin adds one). This is the idea in a few lines.\"\"\"def __init__(self): self.errors = [] def equals(self, actual, expected, msg): if actual != expected: self.errors.append(f\"{msg} expected [{expected}] but found [{actual}]\") def assert_all(self): assertnot self.errors, \"The following asserts failed:\\n\\t\" + \",\\n\\t\".join(self.errors) def test_hard_assert_stops_at_first_failure(): print(\"hard: checking the title\") assert title == \"Shop | Login\", \"page title\"print(\"hard: this line never runs\") def test_soft_assert_collects_every_failure(): soft = SoftAssert() print(\"soft: checking three things\") soft.equals(title, \"Shop | Login\", \"page title\") soft.equals(cart_count, 2, \"cart count\") soft.equals(user, \"admin\", \"user name\") print(\"soft: all three checks ran\") soft.assert_all() def test_other_useful_asserts(): assert user isnotNoneassert\"Shop\"in title, \"title mentions the shop\"assert cart_count != 0assert sorted([\"S\", \"M\", \"L\"]) == sorted([\"L\", \"S\", \"M\"]) Real output of the Python version ============================= test session starts ============================== collecting ... collected 3 items test_prog.py::test_hard_assert_stops_at_first_failure hard: checking the title FAILED test_prog.py::test_soft_assert_collects_every_failure soft: checking three things soft: all three checks ran FAILED test_prog.py::test_other_useful_asserts PASSED =================================== FAILURES =================================== ___________________ test_hard_assert_stops_at_first_failure ____________________ def test_hard_assert_stops_at_first_failure(): print(\"hard: checking the title\") > assert title == \"Shop | Login\", \"page title\" E AssertionError: page title E assert 'Shop | Home' == 'Shop | Login' E E - Shop | Login E + Shop | Home test_prog.py:20: AssertionError ___________________ test_soft_assert_collects_every_failure ____________________ def test_soft_assert_collects_every_failure(): soft = SoftAssert() print(\"soft: checking three things\") soft.equals(title, \"Shop | Login\", \"page title\") soft.equals(cart_count, 2, \"cart count\") soft.equals(user, \"admin\", \"user name\") print(\"soft: all three checks ran\") > soft.assert_all() test_prog.py:31: _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ self = <test_prog.SoftAssert object> def assert_all(self): > assert not self.errors, \"The following asserts failed:\\n\\t\" + \",\\n\\t\".join(self.errors) E AssertionError: The following asserts failed: E page title expected [Shop | Login] but found [Shop | Home], E user name expected [admin] but found [guest] E assert not ['page title expected [Shop | Login] but found [Shop | Home]', 'user name expected [admin] but found [guest]'] E + where ['page title expected [Shop | Login] but found [Shop | Home]', 'user name expected [admin] but found [guest]'] = <test_prog.SoftAssert object>.errors test_prog.py:15: AssertionError =========================== short test summary info ============================ FAILED test_prog.py::test_hard_assert_stops_at_first_failure - AssertionError... FAILED test_prog.py::test_soft_assert_collects_every_failure - AssertionError... ========================= 2 failed, 1 passed in 0.05s ========================== Lesson 5 of 6"],[0,"attributes","Test attributes","Attribute Effect priority = 1 Lower numbers run first. The default is 0, and negative values are allowed enabled = false The test is not run at all and not counted description = \"…\" Text shown in the reports timeOut = 200 Fails the test if it takes longer than 200 milliseconds invocationCount = 3 Runs the same test 3 times (add threadPoolSize to run them in parallel) expectedExceptions = X.class The test passes only if X is thrown. With expectedExceptionsMessageRegExp, the message must match too Without priorities, TestNG runs test methods of a class in alphabetical order, not in the order they are written. Tests should not depend on that order. When order matters, say so with priority or dependsOnMethods. Priority, repeats, a disabled test, a timeout and expected exceptions import time import pytest # pytest runs tests in file order, which plays the role of TestNG's priority.def test_no_priority_runs_first(): print(\"priority 0 (default): noPriorityRunsFirst\") def test_open_home(): \"\"\"Home page opens\"\"\"# a docstring works like descriptionprint(\"priority 1: openHome\") @pytest.mark.parametrize(\"run\", [1, 2, 3]) # like invocationCount = 3def test_refresh_cart(run): print(f\"priority 2: refreshCart run {run} of 3\") @pytest.mark.skip(reason=\"feature not released\") # like enabled = falsedef test_feature_not_released(): print(\"never printed: the test is disabled\") def test_slow_search(): # the pytest-timeout plugin adds @pytest.mark.timeoutprint(\"priority 4: slowSearch sleeps 500 ms with a 200 ms limit\") start = time.time() time.sleep(0.5) elapsed = round(time.time() - start, 1) assert elapsed < 0.2, \"took longer than 200 ms\"def test_divide_by_zero_throws(): print(\"priority 5: expecting ArithmeticException\") with pytest.raises(ZeroDivisionError): 1 / 0def test_rejects_negative_quantity(): with pytest.raises(ValueError, match=\"negative\"): raiseValueError(\"quantity cannot be negative\") Real output of the Python version ============================= test session starts ============================== collecting ... collected 9 items test_prog.py::test_no_priority_runs_first priority 0 (default): noPriorityRunsFirst PASSED test_prog.py::test_open_home priority 1: openHome PASSED test_prog.py::test_refresh_cart[1] priority 2: refreshCart run 1 of 3 PASSED test_prog.py::test_refresh_cart[2] priority 2: refreshCart run 2 of 3 PASSED test_prog.py::test_refresh_cart[3] priority 2: refreshCart run 3 of 3 PASSED test_prog.py::test_feature_not_released SKIPPED (feature not released) test_prog.py::test_slow_search priority 4: slowSearch sleeps 500 ms with a 200 ms limit FAILED test_prog.py::test_divide_by_zero_throws priority 5: expecting ArithmeticException PASSED test_prog.py::test_rejects_negative_quantity PASSED =================================== FAILURES =================================== _______________________________ test_slow_search _______________________________ def test_slow_search(): # the pytest-timeout plugin adds @pytest.mark.timeout print(\"priority 4: slowSearch sleeps 500 ms with a 200 ms limit\") start = time.time() time.sleep(0.5) elapsed = round(time.time() - start, 1) > assert elapsed < 0.2, \"took longer than 200 ms\" E AssertionError: took longer than 200 ms E assert 0.5 < 0.2 test_prog.py:26: AssertionError =========================== short test summary info ============================ FAILED test_prog.py::test_slow_search - AssertionError: took longer than 200 ms ==================== 1 failed, 7 passed, 1 skipped in 0.05s ==================== Lesson 6 of 6"],[0,"dependencies","Dependencies between tests","dependsOnMethods makes a test wait for others and run only if they passed. If a dependency fails, the dependent test is skipped, not failed, so the report shows one real failure instead of a cascade of misleading ones. alwaysRun = true turns it into a soft dependency: run after, but run anyway. 🎬 Animated diagram"],[0,"dependencies","A failed dependency skips what comes after it","Press Play, or step with the arrows, to follow the story one stage at a time. ↔ Swipe sideways to see the whole diagram This is the whole picture. Press ▶ Play to walk through it step by step. 0 / 4 A failed login skips everything that needs it import pytest # pytest has no dependsOnMethods (the pytest-dependency plugin adds it).# This module records results and skips a test when what it needs did not pass. passed = set() def needs(*names): missing = [n for n in names if n notin passed] if missing: pytest.skip(f\"depends on {', '.join(missing)}, which did not pass\") def test_open_browser(): print(\"openBrowser passed\") passed.add(\"open_browser\") def test_login(): needs(\"open_browser\") print(\"login is running\") pytest.fail(\"login button not found\") def test_add_to_cart(): needs(\"login\") print(\"never printed: login failed, so this is skipped\") def test_checkout(): needs(\"add_to_cart\") print(\"never printed: skipped in a chain\") def test_collect_logs(): # no needs() call: it always runs, like alwaysRun = trueprint(\"collectLogs runs anyway because alwaysRun = true\") def test_search_product(): needs(\"open_browser\") print(\"searchProduct passed, it only needs the browser\") Real output of the Python version ============================= test session starts ============================== collecting ... collected 6 items test_prog.py::test_open_browser openBrowser passed PASSED test_prog.py::test_login login is running FAILED test_prog.py::test_add_to_cart SKIPPED (depends on login, which did ...) test_prog.py::test_checkout SKIPPED (depends on add_to_cart, which d...) test_prog.py::test_collect_logs collectLogs runs anyway because alwaysRun = true PASSED test_prog.py::test_search_product searchProduct passed, it only needs the browser PASSED =================================== FAILURES =================================== __________________________________ test_login __________________________________ def test_login(): needs(\"open_browser\") print(\"login is running\") > pytest.fail(\"login button not found\") E Failed: login button not found test_prog.py:19: Failed =========================== short test summary info ============================ FAILED test_prog.py::test_login - Failed: login button not found ==================== 1 failed, 3 passed, 2 skipped in 0.05s ==================== Prefer independent tests. Each one should prepare its own state, for example by logging in through an API in @BeforeMethod. Dependencies make sense for real end to end flows, where a later step truly cannot start without the earlier one. 🧮"],[0,"numbers","Number crunch","Work it out, type your answer and press Check. Stuck? Peek at the hint, then the full working. 0/8correct C1 A class has 4 tests. How many times does @BeforeMethod run? Once per test. Answer: 4 4 C2 A run has 18 passed, 2 failed and 1 skipped. How many tests were run in total? Add them. Answer: 21 18 + 2 + 1 = 21 C1 A test has invocationCount = 3 and the class has 2 other tests. How many test results does the report show? Each invocation is a result. Answer: 5 3 + 2 = 5 C2 Suite: 2 <test> tags × 2 classes × 3 tests. How many @Test runs? Multiply the levels. Answer: 12 2 × 2 × 3 = 12 C1 In the previous suite, how many annotated methods run in total, counting all 9 annotation types (2 suite, 2 per test tag, 2 per class, 3 per test: before, test, after)? Suite 2, test tags 4, classes 8, methods 36. Answer: 50 Suite: 2, test tags: 2 × 2 = 4, classes: 4 × 2 = 8 Methods: 12 × 3 = 36 2 + 4 + 8 + 36 = 50 C2 A pass rate counts only executed tests. With 18 passed, 2 failed and 1 skipped, what is it in percent? Skipped tests did not run. Answer: 90 1818 + 2 × 100 = 90% C1 Boundary values for a length rule of 8 to 16 characters: how many values do you test with min − 1, min, min + 1, max − 1, max, max + 1? 7, 8, 9, 15, 16, 17 Answer: 6 6 values C2 A chain login → addToCart → checkout → invoice. login fails. How many tests are skipped? Everything downstream. Answer: 3 addToCart, checkout and invoice: 3 🎯"],[0,"quiz","Quiz time","Four levels from easy to expert. Code in the questions is labelled with its language. Every answer comes with the reason behind it. 0/16correct Q1 Which annotation marks a method as a test in TestNG? The most common annotation. @Test from org.testng.annotations marks a test method. Q2 Which annotation runs before every single test method? Method level. @BeforeMethod runs before each @Test, @BeforeClass once per class. Q3 What does enabled = false do? Switched off. A disabled test is ignored completely and does not appear in the counts. Q4 In Assert.assertEquals(a, b) in TestNG, which argument is the actual value? TestNG differs from JUnit here. TestNG takes (actual, expected). JUnit takes (expected, actual). Q1 Three tests have no priority: zebra(), apple(), mango(). In which order does TestNG run them? Not the order in the file. Without priorities TestNG sorts methods alphabetically. Q2 A SoftAssert records two failures but assertAll() is never called. The test… Who reports the collected failures? Only assertAll throws. Without it the failures are lost and the test passes. Q3 login() fails. addToCart() has dependsOnMethods = \"login\". What happens to addToCart()? A failed dependency. Dependents of a failed method are marked skipped. Q4 Which pytest feature plays the role of @BeforeMethod and @AfterMethod? Setup before yield, teardown after. A fixture with the default function scope runs around each test. Q1 With 2 <test> tags, 2 classes each and 3 tests per class, how many times does @BeforeClass run? Once per class per <test>. 2 × 2 = 4. Q2 A test has timeOut = 200 and sleeps for 500 ms. What is the result? The limit is a failure condition. TestNG interrupts it and reports a failure because it exceeded 200 ms. Q3 @Test(expectedExceptions = IllegalStateException.class) and the method throws nothing. Result? The exception was expected. The expected exception did not occur, so the test fails. Q4 What does alwaysRun = true do on a test with dependsOnMethods? A soft dependency. It still waits for the dependency to finish, but runs whatever the result. Q1 Priorities: a(-1), b(0), c(0), d(1), e(1, dependsOnMethods = \"f\"), f(2). Which runs last? e must wait for f. Order: a, b, c, d, f, then e, which could not start before f finished. Q2 Why should each test prepare its own state instead of relying on the previous test? Think about rerunning one failed test. Independent tests can be rerun, reordered and parallelised, and their failures point at one cause. Q3 @Test(invocationCount = 5, threadPoolSize = 5) on a method that increments a plain int counter. The final count may be… count++ is not atomic. Parallel threads can read the same value before writing. Use AtomicInteger. Q4 Which statement about @BeforeSuite is true? The outermost layer. Suite level setup runs once at the very beginning. 🏋️"],[0,"quiz","Practice problems","Try each one yourself first. Every solution is written in Python, JavaScript and Java, was run for real, and can be replayed line by line. 0/8solved P1"],[0,"text-utils","Tests for a text utility","I solved it Write tests for reverse, isPalindrome (ignoring case and spaces) and capitalize, including the empty string for each. Example PASSED: capitalizeKeepsEmptyString PASSED: palindromeIgnoresCaseAndSpaces Total tests run: 6, Failures: 0, Skips: 0 One behaviour per test makes a failure easy to locate. Small, named tests act as documentation: each name states a rule of the code. The empty string is the edge case most often forgotten. ⏱️ Time O(1) per test💾 Auxiliary space O(1) Solution: Tests for a text utility import re def reverse(s): return s[::-1] def is_palindrome(s): t = re.sub(r\"[^A-Za-z]\", \"\", s).lower() return t == t[::-1] def capitalize(s): return s[:1].upper() + s[1:] def test_reverses_a_word(): assert reverse(\"selenium\") == \"muineles\"def test_reverses_an_empty_string(): assert reverse(\"\") == \"\"def test_palindrome_ignores_case_and_spaces(): assert is_palindrome(\"Never odd or even\") def test_not_a_palindrome(): assertnot is_palindrome(\"testng\") def test_capitalizes_first_letter(): assert capitalize(\"java\") == \"Java\"def test_capitalize_keeps_empty_string(): assert capitalize(\"\") == \"\" Real output of the Python version ============================= test session starts ============================== collecting ... collected 6 items test_prog.py::test_reverses_a_word PASSED [ 16%] test_prog.py::test_reverses_an_empty_string PASSED [ 33%] test_prog.py::test_palindrome_ignores_case_and_spaces PASSED [ 50%] test_prog.py::test_not_a_palindrome PASSED [ 66%] test_prog.py::test_capitalizes_first_letter PASSED [ 83%] test_prog.py::test_capitalize_keeps_empty_string PASSED [100%] ============================== 6 passed in 0.05s =============================== P2"],[0,"hook-counter","Count how often each hook runs","I solved it Add @BeforeClass, @BeforeMethod, @AfterMethod and @AfterClass to a class with two tests plus one test with invocationCount = 2. Count every call and print the counts in @AfterClass. Example BeforeClass ran 1 time(s) BeforeMethod ran 4 time(s) Test ran 4 time(s) invocationCount = 2 counts as two tests, so the method hooks run around each one. Class level hooks run once, method level hooks once per test run. Counting them is a quick way to confirm your mental model of the lifecycle. ⏱️ Time O(tests)💾 Auxiliary space O(hooks) Solution: Count how often each hook runs import pytest calls = {} def hit(name): calls[name] = calls.get(name, 0) + 1@pytest.fixture(scope=\"module\", autouse=True) def around_class(): hit(\"BeforeClass\") yield hit(\"AfterClass\") for k in sorted(calls): print(f\"\\n{k} ran {calls[k]} time(s)\", end=\"\") print() @pytest.fixture(autouse=True) def around_method(): hit(\"BeforeMethod\") yield hit(\"AfterMethod\") def test_search_by_name(): hit(\"Test\") def test_search_by_category(): hit(\"Test\") @pytest.mark.parametrize(\"run\", [1, 2]) def test_search_empty(run): hit(\"Test\") Real output of the Python version ============================= test session starts ============================== collecting ... collected 4 items test_prog.py::test_search_by_name PASSED test_prog.py::test_search_by_category PASSED test_prog.py::test_search_empty[1] PASSED test_prog.py::test_search_empty[2] PASSED AfterClass ran 1 time(s) AfterMethod ran 4 time(s) BeforeClass ran 1 time(s) BeforeMethod ran 4 time(s) Test ran 4 time(s) ============================== 4 passed in 0.05s =============================== P1"],[0,"cart-tests","Shopping cart tests that find a bug","I solved it Test a Cart: an empty total, adding the same item twice, removing a line, totals below and at the 1000 discount threshold, and rejecting a zero quantity. Use @BeforeMethod for a fresh cart. Example FAILED: discountAppliesAtExactlyOneThousand java.lang.AssertionError: 10% off at 1000 expected [900.0] but found [1000.0] Test exactly 1000, not just 999 and 1001. The code uses > where the rule says \"from 1000\". Only a test at the boundary itself catches it. A fresh cart per test keeps the tests independent. ⏱️ Time O(items)💾 Auxiliary space O(items) Solution: Shopping cart tests that find a bug import pytest classCart: def __init__(self): self.items = {} def add(self, name, price, qty): if qty <= 0: raiseValueError(\"quantity must be positive\") p, q = self.items.get(name, (price, 0)) self.items[name] = (p, q + qty) def remove(self, name): self.items.pop(name, None) def count(self): return sum(q for _, q in self.items.values()) def total(self): t = sum(p * q for p, q in self.items.values()) return t * 0.9if t > 1000else t # bug: the rule says 10% off from 1000 included@pytest.fixture def cart(): # a fresh cart for every test, like @BeforeMethodreturnCart() def test_empty_cart_total_is_zero(cart): assert cart.total() == 0def test_adding_the_same_item_increases_quantity(cart): cart.add(\"Pen\", 25, 2); cart.add(\"Pen\", 25, 3) assert cart.count() == 5def test_remove_deletes_the_line(cart): cart.add(\"Bag\", 899, 1); cart.remove(\"Bag\") assert cart.count() == 0def test_total_below_threshold_has_no_discount(cart): cart.add(\"Book\", 450, 2) assert cart.total() == 900def test_discount_applies_at_exactly_one_thousand(cart): cart.add(\"Shoes\", 500, 2) assert cart.total() == 900, \"10% off at 1000\"def test_zero_quantity_is_rejected(cart): with pytest.raises(ValueError): cart.add(\"Pen\", 25, 0) Real output of the Python version ============================= test session starts ============================== collecting ... collected 6 items test_prog.py::test_empty_cart_total_is_zero PASSED [ 16%] test_prog.py::test_adding_the_same_item_increases_quantity PASSED [ 33%] test_prog.py::test_remove_deletes_the_line PASSED [ 50%] test_prog.py::test_total_below_threshold_has_no_discount PASSED [ 66%] test_prog.py::test_discount_applies_at_exactly_one_thousand FAILED [ 83%] test_prog.py::test_zero_quantity_is_rejected PASSED [100%] =================================== FAILURES =================================== ________________ test_discount_applies_at_exactly_one_thousand _________________ cart = <test_prog.Cart object> def test_discount_applies_at_exactly_one_thousand(cart): cart.add(\"Shoes\", 500, 2) > assert cart.total() == 900, \"10% off at 1000\" E AssertionError: 10% off at 1000 E assert 1000 == 900 E + where 1000 = total() E + where total = <test_prog.Cart object>.total test_prog.py:37: AssertionError =========================== short test summary info ============================ FAILED test_prog.py::test_discount_applies_at_exactly_one_thousand - Assertio... ========================= 1 failed, 5 passed in 0.05s ========================== P2"],[0,"form-soft","Validate a form with soft asserts","I solved it A registration has name, email, phone, age and country. Check all five rules in one test with SoftAssert so that one run reports every invalid field. Example java.lang.AssertionError: The following asserts failed: name needs at least 2 letters, got 'A' ... Put the actual value in each message so the report is useful on its own. With a hard assert you would fix one field, rerun, and find the next. A soft assert shows all four problems at once. ⏱️ Time O(fields)💾 Auxiliary space O(failures) Solution: Validate a form with soft asserts import re classRegistration: name, email, phone, country, age = \"A\", \"asha.example.com\", \"98765\", \"India\", 16def test_every_field_is_valid(): r = Registration() errors = [] # a soft assert is a list of failures checked at the endiflen(r.name) < 2: errors.append(f\"name needs at least 2 letters, got '{r.name}'\") ifnot re.fullmatch(r\"[^@\\s]+@[^@\\s]+\\.\\w+\", r.email): errors.append(f\"email format, got '{r.email}'\") ifnot re.fullmatch(r\"\\d{10}\", r.phone): errors.append(f\"phone needs 10 digits, got '{r.phone}'\") if r.age < 18: errors.append(f\"age must be 18 or more, got {r.age}\") if r.country != \"India\": errors.append(\"country\") assertnot errors, \"The following asserts failed:\\n\" + \"\\n\".join(errors) Real output of the Python version ============================= test session starts ============================== collecting ... collected 1 item test_prog.py::test_every_field_is_valid FAILED [100%] =================================== FAILURES =================================== __________________________ test_every_field_is_valid ___________________________ def test_every_field_is_valid(): r = Registration() errors = [] # a soft assert is a list of failures checked at the end if len(r.name) < 2: errors.append(f\"name needs at least 2 letters, got '{r.name}'\") if not re.fullmatch(r\"[^@\\s]+@[^@\\s]+\\.\\w+\", r.email): errors.append(f\"email format, got '{r.email}'\") if not re.fullmatch(r\"\\d{10}\", r.phone): errors.append(f\"phone needs 10 digits, got '{r.phone}'\") if r.age < 18: errors.append(f\"age must be 18 or more, got {r.age}\") if r.country != \"India\": errors.append(\"country\") > assert not errors, \"The following asserts failed:\\n\" + \"\\n\".join(errors) E AssertionError: The following asserts failed: E name needs at least 2 letters, got 'A' E email format, got 'asha.example.com' E phone needs 10 digits, got '98765' E age must be 18 or more, got 16 E assert not [\"name needs at least 2 letters, got 'A'\", \"email format, got 'asha.example.com'\", \"phone needs 10 digits, got '98765'\", 'age must be 18 or more, got 16'] test_prog.py:16: AssertionError =========================== short test summary info ============================ FAILED test_prog.py::test_every_field_is_valid - AssertionError: The followin... ============================== 1 failed in 0.05s =============================== P1"],[0,"account-exceptions","Expected exceptions on a bank account","I solved it Test withdrawals: normal, the whole balance, an overdraft, a zero amount and a negative opening balance. Check exception types and messages. Include one test whose expectation is wrong to see how TestNG reports it. Example PASSED: overdraftIsRefused FAILED: wrongExpectationFails expectedExceptionsMessageRegExp matches the whole message, so use .* around the key word. Checking the message as well as the type proves the code refused for the right reason. A test that expects an exception fails when nothing is thrown. ⏱️ Time O(1) per test💾 Auxiliary space O(1) Solution: Expected exceptions on a bank account import pytest classAccount: def __init__(self, opening): if opening < 0: raiseValueError(\"opening balance cannot be negative\") self.balance = opening def withdraw(self, amt): if amt <= 0: raiseValueError(\"amount must be positive\") if amt > self.balance: raiseRuntimeError(f\"insufficient funds: balance {self.balance}\") self.balance -= amt @pytest.fixture def acc(): returnAccount(500) def test_withdraw_reduces_balance(acc): acc.withdraw(200); assert acc.balance == 300def test_withdraw_whole_balance(acc): acc.withdraw(500); assert acc.balance == 0def test_overdraft_is_refused(acc): with pytest.raises(RuntimeError, match=\"^insufficient funds\"): acc.withdraw(500.01) def test_zero_amount_is_refused(acc): with pytest.raises(ValueError, match=\"positive\"): acc.withdraw(0) def test_negative_opening_is_refused(): with pytest.raises(ValueError): Account(-1) def test_wrong_expectation_fails(acc): with pytest.raises(RuntimeError): # nothing is raised, so pytest fails this test acc.withdraw(100) Real output of the Python version ============================= test session starts ============================== collecting ... collected 6 items test_prog.py::test_withdraw_reduces_balance PASSED [ 16%] test_prog.py::test_withdraw_whole_balance PASSED [ 33%] test_prog.py::test_overdraft_is_refused PASSED [ 50%] test_prog.py::test_zero_amount_is_refused PASSED [ 66%] test_prog.py::test_negative_opening_is_refused PASSED [ 83%] test_prog.py::test_wrong_expectation_fails FAILED [100%] =================================== FAILURES =================================== _________________________ test_wrong_expectation_fails _________________________ acc = <test_prog.Account object> def test_wrong_expectation_fails(acc): > with pytest.raises(RuntimeError): # nothing is raised, so pytest fails this test ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ E Failed: DID NOT RAISE RuntimeError test_prog.py:35: Failed =========================== short test summary info ============================ FAILED test_prog.py::test_wrong_expectation_fails - Failed: DID NOT RAISE Run... ========================= 1 failed, 5 passed in 0.05s ========================== P2"],[0,"run-order","Predict the run order","I solved it Six tests use priorities −1, 0, 0, 1, 1 and 2, and one has dependsOnMethods. Predict the order, then record the real order with a list printed in @AfterClass. Example 1. login 2. appleSearch 3. zebraSearch 4. addToCart 5. checkout 6. sendInvoice Lowest priority first, ties alphabetical, and a dependent waits for its dependency. sendInvoice has priority 1 but depends on checkout (priority 2), so it moves to the end. Python and JavaScript compute the same order from the rules. ⏱️ Time O(n²) for the simple scheduler💾 Auxiliary space O(n) Solution: Predict the run order # TestNG's rule: lowest priority first, ties in alphabetical order, and a method# with dependsOnMethods waits until what it depends on has run. tests = [(\"checkout\", 2, []), (\"addToCart\", 1, []), (\"zebraSearch\", 0, []), (\"appleSearch\", 0, []), (\"login\", -1, []), (\"sendInvoice\", 1, [\"checkout\"])] def test_predict_run_order(): done, order = set(), [] pending = sorted(tests, key=lambda t: (t[1], t[0])) while pending: for t in pending: if all(d in done for d in t[2]): order.append(t[0]); done.add(t[0]); pending.remove(t) breakfor i, name in enumerate(order, 1): print(f\"\\n{i}. {name}\", end=\"\") print() assert order[-1] == \"sendInvoice\" Real output of the Python version ============================= test session starts ============================== collecting ... collected 1 item test_prog.py::test_predict_run_order 1. login 2. appleSearch 3. zebraSearch 4. addToCart 5. checkout 6. sendInvoice PASSED ============================== 1 passed in 0.05s =============================== P1"],[0,"password-boundaries","Find a boundary bug in a password rule","I solved it The rule says 8 to 16 characters with at least one digit. Write tests for lengths 7, 8, 9, 15, 16 and 17 and for a password without digits. Example FAILED: length16IsAccepted java.lang.AssertionError: 16 characters expected [true] but found [false] Boundary value analysis: min − 1, min, min + 1, max − 1, max, max + 1. The code writes < 16 instead of <= 16. Of the six boundary tests, only the one at exactly 16 fails, which pinpoints the bug. ⏱️ Time O(1) per test💾 Auxiliary space O(1) Solution: Find a boundary bug in a password rule import re import pytest def valid(p): # requirement: 8 to 16 characters, at least one digitreturn8 <= len(p) < 16and re.search(r\"\\d\", p) isnotNone# bug: should be <= 16def of_length(n): return\"a\" * (n - 1) + \"1\"@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"length, expected\", [(7, False), (8, True), (9, True), (15, True), (16, True), (17, False)]) def test_length_boundaries(length, expected): assert valid(of_length(length)) == expected, f\"{length} characters\"def test_no_digit_is_rejected(): assertnot valid(\"abcdefghij\"), \"no digit\" Real output of the Python version ============================= test session starts ============================== collecting ... collected 7 items test_prog.py::test_length_boundaries[7-False] PASSED [ 14%] test_prog.py::test_length_boundaries[8-True] PASSED [ 28%] test_prog.py::test_length_boundaries[9-True] PASSED [ 42%] test_prog.py::test_length_boundaries[15-True] PASSED [ 57%] test_prog.py::test_length_boundaries[16-True] FAILED [ 71%] test_prog.py::test_length_boundaries[17-False] PASSED [ 85%] test_prog.py::test_no_digit_is_rejected PASSED [100%] =================================== FAILURES =================================== _______________________ test_length_boundaries[16-True] ________________________ length = 16, expected = True @pytest.mark.parametrize(\"length, expected\", [(7, False), (8, True), (9, True), (15, True), (16, True), (17, False)]) def test_length_boundaries(length, expected): > assert valid(of_length(length)) == expected, f\"{length} characters\" E AssertionError: 16 characters E assert False == True E + where False = valid('aaaaaaaaaaaaaaa1') E + where 'aaaaaaaaaaaaaaa1' = of_length(16) test_prog.py:15: AssertionError =========================== short test summary info ============================ FAILED test_prog.py::test_length_boundaries[16-True] - AssertionError: 16 cha... ========================= 1 failed, 6 passed in 0.05s ========================== P2"],[0,"lifecycle-table","A table driven multiplication test","I solved it Write one test method that checks five multiplication facts, including zero and a negative factor, fed from a table of rows. This previews the data provider on the next page. Example PASSED: multiplies(7, 9, 63) PASSED: multiplies(-3, 4, -12) Each row holds both inputs and the expected product. One method, many rows: adding a case means adding a row, not a method. The report lists every row with its arguments. ⏱️ Time O(rows)💾 Auxiliary space O(rows) Solution: A table driven multiplication test import pytest @pytest.mark.parametrize(\"a, b, expected\", [(7, 1, 7), (7, 9, 63), (12, 12, 144), (0, 5, 0), (-3, 4, -12)]) def test_multiplies(a, b, expected): assert a * b == expected, f\"{a} x {b}\" Real output of the Python version ============================= test session starts ============================== collecting ... collected 5 items test_prog.py::test_multiplies[7-1-7] PASSED [ 20%] test_prog.py::test_multiplies[7-9-63] PASSED [ 40%] test_prog.py::test_multiplies[12-12-144] PASSED [ 60%] test_prog.py::test_multiplies[0-5-0] PASSED [ 80%] test_prog.py::test_multiplies[-3-4--12] PASSED [100%] ============================== 5 passed in 0.05s ==============================="],[0,"lifecycle-table","Summary","The one idea to keep from each lesson. Open a lesson again when a line does not make sense yet. Why a test frameworkTest classes go in src/test/java. Run them from the IDE (right click, Run), from a testng.xml file, or with mvn test, where the Maven Surefire plugin finds the tests. A first test classA test method is a public void method marked @Test. It calls the code under test and checks the result with an assertion. If every assertion holds, the test passes.Remember: TestNG's Assert.assertEquals(actual, expected) takes the actual value first. JUnit uses the opposite order. Swapping them still passes and fails correctly, but the failure message then says \"expected 3 but found 4\", which sends you looking in the wrong place. The annotation lifecycle Hard and soft assertions Test attributesWithout priorities, TestNG runs test methods of a class in alphabetical order, not in the order they are written. Tests should not depend on that order. When order matters, say so with priority or dependsOnMethods. Dependencies between testsdependsOnMethods makes a test wait for others and run only if they passed. If a dependency fails, the dependent test is skipped, not failed, so the report shows one real failure instead of a cascade of misleading ones."],[0,"comic","Annotations do the bookkeeping","Tap any panel to flip it for the deeper idea, or press Play the comic. 📌 Remember Write independent tests, assert with clear messages, and let the annotations handle setup and reporting."],[0,"real-life","Real life, just funnier","The same idea is hiding in things you use every day. 🧑‍🍳A restaurant kitchen Before service the kitchen is set up once (suite), each table gets fresh cutlery (method), and the dishwasher runs at closing (after suite). Annotations schedule the same layers for tests. 📋An inspection checklist A hard check stops the inspection: no fire exit, no point checking the paint. A soft check writes down every scratch and dent, then hands over the full list at the end. 🚆Train connections If the first train is cancelled, you never board the second one. The second leg is skipped, not failed. That is dependsOnMethods. Hi, I am MiraI will read \"TestNG Foundations: Annotations, Assertions and Test Control\" to you 🐢🐇 Part 1 of 8Welcome A main method can run checks, but it cannot report which passed, skip the rest when setup fails, or rerun only the failures. TestNG does all of that through annotations. Every example here runs on real TestNG, with the same idea in pytest and Mocha beside it. Voice settings Voice In English, read the full notes of each part instead of the short summary Scroll the page along with me ⚠️ No English voice is installed on this device, so your browser may read in another accent or stay silent. Microsoft Edge offers free online English voices, or you can add one in your device's speech settings. The captions above are always there to read along."],[1,"","Suites, Groups, Parameters and Data Providers","Organising and feeding tests with TestNG: the structure of testng.xml with suite, test, classes, methods, include and exclude, groups for smoke and regression runs, @Parameters with suite and test level values and @Optional, data providers with Object[][], named providers, Iterator providers that read files, indices, and parallel execution with thread-count and thread safe code. · testng.xml · suite · test tag · classes · class · methods · include · exclude · packages · groups · smoke · regression · sanity · @BeforeGroups · dependsOnGroups · @Parameters · parameter · @Optional · suite level parameter · test level parameter · cross browser · @DataProvider · Object[][] · dataProvider name · Iterator<Object[]> · indices · data driven · CSV data provider · JSON data provider · parallel · thread-count · parallel methods · parallel classes · parallel tests · ThreadLocal · AtomicInteger · pytest markers · pytest -m · pytest -k · parametrize · mocha --grep ✅ Brainy Bytes · TestNG ✅ TestNG›🧪 TestNG Framework›Suites and data 🧾 Step 1 · Topic 2 of 3 · Beginner to advanced"],[1,"top","","Once a project has hundreds of tests, the questions change: which tests run tonight, on which browser, with which data, and how fast. TestNG answers them outside the test code, in testng.xml, groups, parameters and data providers. Every Java example on this page runs on real TestNG with the suite file shown beside it. ⏱️ About 8 min read📚 6 parts🎬 2 animations🏋️ 8 practice problems🎯 16 quiz questions🧮 8 calculationsJava with real TestNG, with pytest (Python) and Mocha (JavaScript) side by sideThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1The testng.xml suite file 2Groups for smoke and regression runs 3Parameters from testng.xml 4Data providers 5Data providers that read files 6Parallel execution Lesson 1 of 6"],[1,"suite-xml","The testng.xml suite file","testng.xml describes a run without touching the test code: which classes, which methods, in which <test> blocks. One suite holds one or more tests, each test holds classes, and each class can include or exclude methods. <!DOCTYPE suite SYSTEM \"http://testng.org/testng-1.0.dtd\"> <suite name=\"Shop regression\" verbose=\"1\"> <test name=\"Login checks\"> <classes> <class name=\"LoginTests\"> <methods> <exclude name=\"forgotPassword\"/> <!-- not ready in this build --> </methods> </class> </classes> </test> <test name=\"Cart checks\"> <classes> <class name=\"CartTests\"> <methods> <include name=\"addItem\"/> <include name=\"removeItem\"/> </methods> </class> </classes> </test> </suite> 🎬 Animated diagram"],[1,"suite-xml","How testng.xml nests","Press Play, or step with the arrows, to follow the story one stage at a time. ↔ Swipe sideways to see the whole diagram This is the whole picture. Press ▶ Play to walk through it step by step. 0 / 4 Running the suite above: one excluded method, two included ones # pytest picks tests by file, class and name. The -k option and node IDs play the role of testng.xml.# This run uses: pytest -v -s -k \"not forgot_password and not apply_coupon\"classTestLogin: def test_valid_login(self): print(\"LoginTests.validLogin\") def test_invalid_password(self): print(\"LoginTests.invalidPassword\") def test_forgot_password(self): print(\"LoginTests.forgotPassword\") classTestCart: def test_add_item(self): print(\"CartTests.addItem\") def test_remove_item(self): print(\"CartTests.removeItem\") def test_apply_coupon(self): print(\"CartTests.applyCoupon\") Real output of the Python version ============================= test session starts ============================== collecting ... collected 6 items / 2 deselected / 4 selected test_prog.py::TestLogin::test_valid_login LoginTests.validLogin PASSED test_prog.py::TestLogin::test_invalid_password LoginTests.invalidPassword PASSED test_prog.py::TestCart::test_add_item CartTests.addItem PASSED test_prog.py::TestCart::test_remove_item CartTests.removeItem PASSED ======================= 4 passed, 2 deselected in 0.05s ======================== Classes are named with their package, for example com.shop.tests.LoginTests. <packages><package name=\"com.shop.tests\"/></packages> runs every class in a package. With Maven, point the Surefire plugin at the file with <suiteXmlFile>testng.xml</suiteXmlFile>. Lesson 2 of 6"],[1,"groups","Groups for smoke and regression runs","A test can belong to several groups: @Test(groups = {\"smoke\", \"login\"}). The suite file then runs a slice of the project, such as every smoke test except the ones known to be broken. Exclude always wins over include. <test name=\"Smoke\"> <groups> <run> <include name=\"smoke\"/> <exclude name=\"broken\"/> </run> </groups> <classes><class name=\"GroupTests\"/></classes> </test> 🎬 Try it"],[1,"groups","Include and exclude groups","Tick groups to include and to exclude, as in the <run> block of testng.xml. A test runs when it belongs to at least one included group and to no excluded group. Exclude always wins. With nothing included, every test is a candidate. Include smoke regression login cart slow broken Exclude smoke regression login cart slow broken <groups> <run> <include name=\"smoke\"/> <exclude name=\"broken\"/> </run> </groups> ▶ validLoginsmokeloginincluded by smoke · lockedUserregressionloginin no included group ▶ addToCartsmokecartincluded by smoke · couponExpiredregressioncartin no included group · searchSuggestionssmokebrokenexcluded by broken · fullCatalogueregressionslowin no included group · bulkImportslowin no included group · healthCheckno groupin no included group 2 of 8 tests run. A test with no group runs only when the include list is empty. A smoke run that leaves out broken tests import pytest # pytest markers play the role of TestNG groups. This run uses: pytest -m \"smoke and not broken\"# The markers are registered in pytest.ini so pytest does not warn about unknown marks.@pytest.fixture(scope=\"module\") def seed_data(): print(\"\\n@BeforeGroups smoke: seed test data once\") @pytest.mark.smoke @pytest.mark.login def test_valid_login(seed_data): print(\"validLogin smoke, login\") @pytest.mark.regression @pytest.mark.login def test_locked_user(): print(\"lockedUser regression, login\") @pytest.mark.smoke @pytest.mark.cart def test_add_to_cart(seed_data): print(\"addToCart smoke, cart\") @pytest.mark.regression @pytest.mark.cart def test_coupon_expired(): print(\"couponExpired regression, cart\") @pytest.mark.smoke @pytest.mark.broken def test_search_suggestions(seed_data): print(\"searchSuggestions smoke, broken\") @pytest.mark.smoke def test_checkout(seed_data): print(\"checkout smoke\") Real output of the Python version ============================= test session starts ============================== collecting ... collected 6 items / 3 deselected / 3 selected test_prog.py::test_valid_login @BeforeGroups smoke: seed test data once validLogin smoke, login PASSED test_prog.py::test_add_to_cart addToCart smoke, cart PASSED test_prog.py::test_checkout checkout smoke PASSED ======================= 3 passed, 3 deselected in 0.05s ======================== Configuration methods such as @BeforeMethod and @BeforeGroups belong to no group by default, so a group filter leaves them out. Add alwaysRun = true to setup methods, or give them the same groups, or your tests will run without their setup. Lesson 3 of 6"],[1,"parameters","Parameters from testng.xml","@Parameters({\"browser\", \"baseUrl\"}) fills method arguments from <parameter> tags. A parameter in a <test> overrides the same name at suite level, so one suite can run the same class on Chrome and Firefox. Values are written as text, and TestNG converts them to simple types such as int when the method asks for one. @Optional(\"30\") supplies a default when the xml has no such parameter. <suite name=\"Cross browser\"> <parameter name=\"baseUrl\" value=\"https://qa.example.com\"/> <!-- suite level --> <parameter name=\"username\" value=\"qa_user\"/> <test name=\"Chrome\"> <parameter name=\"browser\" value=\"chrome\"/> <!-- test level --> <classes><class name=\"ParameterTests\"/></classes> </test> <test name=\"Firefox\"> <parameter name=\"browser\" value=\"firefox\"/> <classes><class name=\"ParameterTests\"/></classes> </test> </suite> One class, two browsers, a default timeout import pytest # TestNG reads <parameter> values from testng.xml. pytest usually reads them from the command line# (pytest --browser chrome, added in conftest.py) or from environment variables. Here a fixture# returns the values for two <test> tags, so each test runs once per browser.CONFIG = [{\"browser\": \"chrome\", \"baseUrl\": \"https://qa.example.com\", \"username\": \"qa_user\"}, {\"browser\": \"firefox\", \"baseUrl\": \"https://qa.example.com\", \"username\": \"qa_user\"}] @pytest.fixture(params=CONFIG, ids=[\"chrome\", \"firefox\"]) def env(request): return request.param def test_open_home(env): print(f\"open {env['baseUrl']} in {env['browser']}\") def test_login(env): timeout = int(env.get(\"timeout\", \"30\")) # the default plays the role of @Optional(\"30\")print(f\"login as {env['username']} with a {timeout} s wait\") Real output of the Python version ============================= test session starts ============================== collecting ... collected 4 items test_prog.py::test_open_home[chrome] open https://qa.example.com in chrome PASSED test_prog.py::test_open_home[firefox] open https://qa.example.com in firefox PASSED test_prog.py::test_login[chrome] login as qa_user with a 30 s wait PASSED test_prog.py::test_login[firefox] login as qa_user with a 30 s wait PASSED ============================== 4 passed in 0.05s =============================== Lesson 4 of 6"],[1,"data-provider","Data providers","A data provider is a method that returns rows of arguments. TestNG calls the test once per row and reports each run with its arguments, so a failure names the exact data that broke it. @Parameters A few fixed settings from testng.xml, such as the browser or the URL. One value per name. @DataProvider Many rows of test data from code or files. The same test runs once for every row. Five login rows, one of them finds a case sensitivity question import pytest def login(user, password): # stand-in for the real login pageifnot user ornot password: return\"required\"return\"welcome\"if user == \"admin\"and password == \"Admin@123\"else\"invalid\"ROWS = [(\"admin\", \"Admin@123\", \"welcome\"), (\"admin\", \"wrong\", \"invalid\"), (\"\", \"Admin@123\", \"required\"), (\"guest\", \"\", \"required\"), (\"ADMIN\", \"Admin@123\", \"welcome\")] # is the user name case sensitive?@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"user, password, expected\", ROWS) # pytest's data providerdef test_login(user, password, expected): print(f'login(\"{user}\", \"{password}\") expecting {expected}') assert login(user, password) == expected Real output of the Python version ============================= test session starts ============================== collecting ... collected 5 items test_prog.py::test_login[admin-Admin@123-welcome] login(\"admin\", \"Admin@123\") expecting welcome PASSED test_prog.py::test_login[admin-wrong-invalid] login(\"admin\", \"wrong\") expecting invalid PASSED test_prog.py::test_login[-Admin@123-required] login(\"\", \"Admin@123\") expecting required PASSED test_prog.py::test_login[guest--required] login(\"guest\", \"\") expecting required PASSED test_prog.py::test_login[ADMIN-Admin@123-welcome] login(\"ADMIN\", \"Admin@123\") expecting welcome FAILED =================================== FAILURES =================================== _____________________ test_login[ADMIN-Admin@123-welcome] ______________________ user = 'ADMIN', password = 'Admin@123', expected = 'welcome' @pytest.mark.parametrize(\"user, password, expected\", ROWS) # pytest's data provider def test_login(user, password, expected): print(f'login(\"{user}\", \"{password}\") expecting {expected}') > assert login(user, password) == expected E AssertionError: assert 'invalid' == 'welcome' E E - welcome E + invalid test_prog.py:20: AssertionError =========================== short test summary info ============================ FAILED test_prog.py::test_login[ADMIN-Admin@123-welcome] - AssertionError: as... ========================= 1 failed, 4 passed in 0.05s ========================== pytest's @pytest.mark.parametrize is the same idea, and each row appears in the report as test_login[admin-wrong-invalid]. Mocha has no built in equivalent, so tests loop over the rows and create one it() per row. Lesson 5 of 6"],[1,"provider-files","Data providers that read files",", lazy loading, CSV, JSON, Excel, Method parameter, provider for several tests, indices, rerun selected rows, dataProviderClass\"> A provider can return an Iterator<Object[]> instead of an array, so rows are produced one at a time. It can also take a Method argument to know which test is asking, and live in a separate class shared through dataProviderClass. The file readers from the Java chapter plug straight in. src/test/resources/search.csv term,minResults laptop,10 usb c cable,20 ,1 phone,15 Search terms from a CSV file, one blank term fails import csv import pytest def rows(): print(\"provider called for test_search_shows_results\") with open(\"src/test/resources/search.csv\", newline=\"\") as f: return [tuple(r) for r in list(csv.reader(f))[1:]] def results_for(term): # stand-in for counting results on the pagereturn0ifnot term.strip() elselen(term) * 3@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"term, min_results\", rows()) def test_search_shows_results(term, min_results): found = results_for(term) print(f\"search '{term}' found {found}, need at least {min_results}\") assert found >= int(min_results), f\"results for '{term}'\" Real output of the Python version ============================= test session starts ============================== collecting ... provider called for test_search_shows_results collected 4 items test_prog.py::test_search_shows_results[laptop-10] search 'laptop' found 18, need at least 10 PASSED test_prog.py::test_search_shows_results[usb c cable-20] search 'usb c cable' found 33, need at least 20 PASSED test_prog.py::test_search_shows_results[ -1] search ' ' found 0, need at least 1 FAILED test_prog.py::test_search_shows_results[phone-15] search 'phone' found 15, need at least 15 PASSED =================================== FAILURES =================================== ________________________ test_search_shows_results[ -1] ________________________ term = ' ', min_results = '1' @pytest.mark.parametrize(\"term, min_results\", rows()) def test_search_shows_results(term, min_results): found = results_for(term) print(f\"search '{term}' found {found}, need at least {min_results}\") > assert found >= int(min_results), f\"results for '{term}'\" E AssertionError: results for ' ' E assert 0 >= 1 E + where 1 = int('1') test_prog.py:19: AssertionError =========================== short test summary info ============================ FAILED test_prog.py::test_search_shows_results[ -1] - AssertionError: results... ========================= 1 failed, 3 passed in 0.05s ========================== Lesson 6 of 6"],[1,"parallel","Parallel execution","parallel= What runs at the same time \"methods\" Test methods, even from the same class \"classes\" Whole classes, methods inside a class stay sequential \"tests\" Each <test> block, for example one per browser \"instances\" Instances of the same class, useful with @Factory <suite name=\"Parallel run\" parallel=\"methods\" thread-count=\"4\"> <test name=\"Shop\"><classes><class name=\"ParallelTests\"/></classes></test> </suite> Four 300 ms tests finish in about 300 ms # pytest runs tests one after another. The pytest-xdist plugin adds parallel runs: pytest -n 4.# This sketch shows the same idea with a thread pool, which is what parallel=\"methods\" does.import threading import time from concurrent.futures importThreadPoolExecutor threads, finished, lock = set(), [0], threading.Lock() def work(name): with lock: threads.add(threading.current_thread().name) time.sleep(0.3) # pretend each test drives a browser for 300 mswith lock: finished[0] += 1def test_run_four_tests_in_parallel(): start = time.time() withThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=4) as pool: list(pool.map(work, [\"login\", \"search\", \"cart\", \"checkout\"])) took = time.time() - start print(f\"\\ntests finished: {finished[0]}\") print(f\"worker threads used: {len(threads)}\") print(f\"finished in under 1 second: {str(took < 1).lower()} (one after another would take 1.2 s)\") assert finished[0] == 4 Real output of the Python version ============================= test session starts ============================== collecting ... collected 1 item test_prog.py::test_run_four_tests_in_parallel tests finished: 4 worker threads used: 4 finished in under 1 second: true (one after another would take 1.2 s) PASSED ============================== 1 passed in 0.05s =============================== Parallel tests must not share changeable state. A plain int counter or a single WebDriver field shared by threads gives random failures. Use AtomicInteger and concurrent collections for counters, and ThreadLocal<WebDriver> so each thread drives its own browser. 🧮"],[1,"numbers","Number crunch","Work it out, type your answer and press Check. Stuck? Peek at the hint, then the full working. 0/8correct C1 A suite has 2 <test> tags, each with the same class of 3 tests. How many test runs? Tags × tests Answer: 6 2 × 3 = 6 C2 A provider has 8 rows with indices = {1, 3, 5}. How many runs? Count the indices. Answer: 3 3 runs C1 Two providers feed one test each: 5 valid emails and 6 invalid ones. How many runs in total? Add the rows. Answer: 11 5 + 6 = 11 C2 12 tests of 2 seconds each run with thread-count 4 and parallel methods. About how many seconds does the run take? Total work ÷ threads Answer: 6 12 × 24 = 6 seconds, ignoring overhead C1 All combinations of 3 browsers, 3 operating systems and 2 languages: how many? Multiply the choices. Answer: 18 3 × 3 × 2 = 18 C2 How many value pairs must a pairwise set cover for factors of size 3, 3 and 2? Browser × OS, browser × language, OS × language. Answer: 21 9 + 6 + 6 = 21 pairs C1 Each full combination covers 3 pairs. What is the minimum possible number of runs to cover 21 pairs? At least 21 ÷ 3, and at least 3 × 3 to cover every browser and OS pair. Answer: 9 21 ÷ 3 = 7, but the 9 browser and OS pairs each need their own run So at least 9 runs, which the greedy method reaches C2 A suite of 400 tests averaging 30 s runs on 8 threads. About how many minutes does it take? Total seconds ÷ threads ÷ 60 Answer: 25 400 × 308 × 60 = 25 minutes 🎯"],[1,"quiz","Quiz time","Four levels from easy to expert. Code in the questions is labelled with its language. Every answer comes with the reason behind it. 0/16correct Q1 What is the outermost tag of testng.xml? It holds everything else. A suite contains test tags, which contain classes. Q2 What does a @DataProvider method return? Rows of arguments. Each inner array is one set of arguments for one run. Q3 Which annotation reads values from <parameter> tags? Same word as the tag. @Parameters({\"browser\"}) fills the argument from <parameter name=\"browser\" ...>. Q4 Which pytest option selects tests by marker, like TestNG groups? m for marker. pytest -m \"smoke and not broken\" selects by markers. -k selects by name. Q1 A test is in groups smoke and broken. The suite includes smoke and excludes broken. Does it run? Which one wins? Exclude always wins over include. Q2 A parameter browser is set to chrome at suite level and firefox inside a <test>. What does that test receive? The closer level wins. Test level values override suite level values with the same name. Q3 In testng.xml, <parameter name=\"timeout\" value=\"30\"/> is written as… XML attributes are text. The value is text in the file. TestNG converts it for simple parameter types such as int and boolean. Q4 A provider returns 5 rows. How many times does the test method run? Once per row. Each row is one invocation with its own report entry. Q1 A suite filters by group smoke. @BeforeMethod has no groups and no alwaysRun. What happens? Configuration methods are filtered too. Add alwaysRun = true or the same group to setup methods used in group runs. Q2 Why return Iterator<Object[]> instead of Object[][] from a provider? Lazy loading. An iterator hands rows to TestNG as needed. Q3 What does @DataProvider(indices = {0, 2}) do? Zero based positions. Only the listed rows are passed to the test, handy for rerunning failures. Q4 parallel=\"classes\" means… The unit of parallelism is the class. Each class gets a thread, which keeps per class state safe. Q1 Four tests each increment a shared plain int counter in parallel. Which change makes the count reliable? count++ is three steps. AtomicInteger performs the read, add and write as one indivisible step. Q2 Why use ThreadLocal<WebDriver> in a parallel suite? One browser per thread. A single shared driver field would be used by several threads at once. Q3 3 browsers × 3 operating systems × 2 languages give 18 combinations. What does pairwise testing guarantee? Pairs, not triples. Most defects involve one or two factors, so covering all pairs catches most of them with far fewer runs. Q4 A data row in a JSON file has a wrong expected value. What does the failure message help you decide? Read the whole message. With the arguments and both values in view, you can check the rule by hand and fix the right side. 🏋️"],[1,"quiz","Practice problems","Try each one yourself first. Every solution is written in Python, JavaScript and Java, was run for real, and can be replayed line by line. 0/8solved P1"],[1,"email-rule","Valid and invalid email providers","I solved it Write two named data providers, one with valid and one with invalid email addresses, and two tests that use them. The rule under test has a bug. Example PASSED: rejects(\"plainaddress\") FAILED: rejects(\"asha@example\") Name each provider and point each test at it with dataProvider = \"…\". Separate positive and negative providers keep each test's expectation simple. The address without a dot in the domain slips through because the regex makes the dot part optional. ⏱️ Time O(rows)💾 Auxiliary space O(rows) Solution: Valid and invalid email providers import re import pytest def valid(e): return re.fullmatch(r\"[\\w.+-]+@[\\w-]+(\\.[\\w-]+)*\", e) isnotNone# bug: the dot part is optional@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"email\", [\"asha@example.com\", \"ravi.k+qa@mail.co.in\", \"x_1@test-site.org\"]) def test_accepts(email): assert valid(email), email @pytest.mark.parametrize(\"email\", [\"plainaddress\", \"@example.com\", \"asha@\", \"asha@example\", \"a b@example.com\"]) def test_rejects(email): assertnot valid(email), email Real output of the Python version ============================= test session starts ============================== collecting ... collected 8 items test_prog.py::test_accepts[asha@example.com] PASSED [ 12%] test_prog.py::test_accepts[ravi.k+qa@mail.co.in] PASSED [ 25%] test_prog.py::test_accepts[x_1@test-site.org] PASSED [ 37%] test_prog.py::test_rejects[plainaddress] PASSED [ 50%] test_prog.py::test_rejects[@example.com] PASSED [ 62%] test_prog.py::test_rejects[asha@] PASSED [ 75%] test_prog.py::test_rejects[asha@example] FAILED [ 87%] test_prog.py::test_rejects[a b@example.com] PASSED [100%] =================================== FAILURES =================================== __________________________ test_rejects[asha@example] __________________________ email = 'asha@example' @pytest.mark.parametrize(\"email\", [\"plainaddress\", \"@example.com\", \"asha@\", \"asha@example\", \"a b@example.com\"]) def test_rejects(email): > assert not valid(email), email E AssertionError: asha@example E assert not True E + where True = valid('asha@example') test_prog.py:16: AssertionError =========================== short test summary info ============================ FAILED test_prog.py::test_rejects[asha@example] - AssertionError: asha@example ========================= 1 failed, 7 passed in 0.05s ========================== P2"],[1,"group-run","Regression without slow tests","I solved it Seven tests carry groups sanity, regression, slow and payment. Write a testng.xml that runs regression but not slow, and predict which tests run before running it. Example cardPayment filtersWork loginWorks Total tests run: 3, Failures: 0, Skips: 0 A test with no group never matches an include list. fullCatalogue is in regression but also slow, so the exclude removes it. noGroupAtAll and the sanity only test are not in regression. ⏱️ Time O(tests)💾 Auxiliary space O(1) Solution: Regression without slow tests import pytest # Run with: pytest -m \"regression and not slow\"@pytest.mark.sanity def test_home_loads(): print(\"homeLoads\") @pytest.mark.sanity @pytest.mark.regression def test_login_works(): print(\"loginWorks\") @pytest.mark.regression def test_filters_work(): print(\"filtersWork\") @pytest.mark.regression @pytest.mark.slow def test_full_catalogue(): print(\"fullCatalogue\") @pytest.mark.regression @pytest.mark.payment def test_card_payment(): print(\"cardPayment\") @pytest.mark.slow def test_bulk_import(): print(\"bulkImport\") def test_no_group_at_all(): print(\"noGroupAtAll\") Real output of the Python version ============================= test session starts ============================== collecting ... collected 7 items / 4 deselected / 3 selected test_prog.py::test_login_works loginWorks PASSED test_prog.py::test_filters_work filtersWork PASSED test_prog.py::test_card_payment cardPayment PASSED ======================= 3 passed, 4 deselected in 0.05s ======================== P1"],[1,"cross-browser","Layout on three browsers","I solved it Use @Parameters for browser and width, and a testng.xml with three <test> blocks, to check which layout each screen width gets. Example chrome 1366 px -> desktop grid safari 390 px -> mobile menu Width arrives as a String, parse it first. The class never names a browser. Adding a fourth target is a change to the xml only. ⏱️ Time O(targets)💾 Auxiliary space O(1) Solution: Layout on three browsers import pytest TARGETS = [(\"chrome\", 1366), (\"firefox\", 1024), (\"safari\", 390)] @pytest.mark.parametrize(\"browser, width\", TARGETS, ids=[t[0] for t inTARGETS]) def test_layout_fits(browser, width): layout = \"mobile menu\"if width < 768else\"tablet grid\"if width < 1200else\"desktop grid\"print(f\"{browser:<8} {width:5d} px -> {layout}\") Real output of the Python version ============================= test session starts ============================== collecting ... collected 3 items test_prog.py::test_layout_fits[chrome] chrome 1366 px -> desktop grid PASSED test_prog.py::test_layout_fits[firefox] firefox 1024 px -> tablet grid PASSED test_prog.py::test_layout_fits[safari] safari 390 px -> mobile menu PASSED ============================== 3 passed in 0.05s =============================== P2"],[1,"rerun-rows","Rerun selected rows with indices","I solved it A coupon provider has five rows. Use indices to run only rows 0, 2 and 4, as you would after a failure in those rows. Example PASSED: couponTotal(\"SAVE10\", 1000, 900.0) PASSED: couponTotal(\"FREESHIP\", 450, 450.0) Indices are zero based positions in the returned array. Indices let you rerun just the rows that failed without editing the data. pytest does the same by slicing the list passed to parametrize. ⏱️ Time O(selected rows)💾 Auxiliary space O(rows) Solution: Rerun selected rows with indices import pytest ROWS = [(\"SAVE10\", 1000, 900.0), (\"SAVE20\", 1000, 800.0), (\"FLAT50\", 300, 250.0), (\"EXPIRED\", 500, 500.0), (\"FREESHIP\", 450, 450.0)] PICK = [0, 2, 4] # like indices = {0, 2, 4}def apply(code, amount): return {\"SAVE10\": amount * 0.9, \"SAVE20\": amount * 0.8, \"FLAT50\": amount - 50}.get(code, amount) @pytest.mark.parametrize(\"code, amount, expected\", [ROWS[i] for i inPICK]) def test_coupon_total(code, amount, expected): print(f\"{code:<8} on {amount:4d} -> {float(apply(code, amount)):.1f} (expected {expected:.1f})\") assert apply(code, amount) == pytest.approx(expected) Real output of the Python version ============================= test session starts ============================== collecting ... collected 3 items test_prog.py::test_coupon_total[SAVE10-1000-900.0] SAVE10 on 1000 -> 900.0 (expected 900.0) PASSED test_prog.py::test_coupon_total[FLAT50-300-250.0] FLAT50 on 300 -> 250.0 (expected 250.0) PASSED test_prog.py::test_coupon_total[FREESHIP-450-450.0] FREESHIP on 450 -> 450.0 (expected 450.0) PASSED ============================== 3 passed in 0.05s =============================== P1"],[1,"json-provider","Shipping rules from a JSON file","I solved it Read shipping test cases (weight, zone, expected cost) from a JSON file with Jackson inside a data provider. One row in the file has a wrong expected value. Example FAILED: shippingCost(1.2, \"local\", 40.0) java.lang.AssertionError: 1.2 kg local expected [40.0] but found [60.0] Check the rule by hand for the failing row before changing any code. The rule charges 20 per started kilogram above 1, so 1.2 kg local costs 40 + 20 = 60. Here the data is wrong, not the code. The failure message, with arguments and both values, is what lets you tell. ⏱️ Time O(rows)💾 Auxiliary space O(rows) Solution: Shipping rules from a JSON file import json import math import pytest def shipping(weight_kg, zone): base = {\"local\": 40, \"national\": 80}.get(zone, 250) return base if weight_kg <= 1else base + math.ceil(weight_kg - 1) * 20with open(\"src/test/resources/shipping.json\") as f: CASES = [(c[\"weight\"], c[\"zone\"], c[\"expected\"]) for c in json.load(f)[\"cases\"]] @pytest.mark.parametrize(\"weight, zone, expected\", CASES) def test_shipping_cost(weight, zone, expected): assert shipping(weight, zone) == pytest.approx(expected), f\"{weight} kg {zone}\" Real output of the Python version ============================= test session starts ============================== collecting ... collected 5 items test_prog.py::test_shipping_cost[0.5-local-40] PASSED [ 20%] test_prog.py::test_shipping_cost[1.0-national-80] PASSED [ 40%] test_prog.py::test_shipping_cost[2.5-national-120] PASSED [ 60%] test_prog.py::test_shipping_cost[3.0-international-290] PASSED [ 80%] test_prog.py::test_shipping_cost[1.2-local-40] FAILED [100%] =================================== FAILURES =================================== _______________________ test_shipping_cost[1.2-local-40] _______________________ weight = 1.2, zone = 'local', expected = 40 @pytest.mark.parametrize(\"weight, zone, expected\", CASES) def test_shipping_cost(weight, zone, expected): > assert shipping(weight, zone) == pytest.approx(expected), f\"{weight} kg {zone}\" E AssertionError: 1.2 kg local E assert 60 == 40 ± 4.0e-05 E E comparison failed E Obtained: 60 E Expected: 40 ± 4.0e-05 test_prog.py:17: AssertionError =========================== short test summary info ============================ FAILED test_prog.py::test_shipping_cost[1.2-local-40] - AssertionError: 1.2 k... ========================= 1 failed, 4 passed in 0.05s ========================== P2"],[1,"shared-provider","A provider class shared by two tests","I solved it Put one provider in a separate TestData class and use it from two tests with dataProviderClass. Give the provider a Method argument so admins and customers get different rows. Example PASSED: adminHasFullAccess(\"root\", 3) PASSED: customerAccessLevel(\"meena\", 2) A provider in another class must be static. Central test data avoids copies drifting apart across test classes. The Method argument lets one provider serve related tests. ⏱️ Time O(rows)💾 Auxiliary space O(rows) Solution: A provider class shared by two tests # Shared test data usually lives in conftest.py or a data module that every test file imports.import pytest ADMINS = [(\"root\", 3), (\"ops\", 3)] CUSTOMERS = [(\"asha\", 1), (\"ravi\", 1), (\"meena\", 2)] def access_level(user): return {\"root\": 3, \"ops\": 3, \"meena\": 2}.get(user, 1) @pytest.mark.parametrize(\"user, level\", ADMINS) def test_admin_has_full_access(user, level): assert access_level(user) == level, user @pytest.mark.parametrize(\"user, level\", CUSTOMERS) def test_customer_access_level(user, level): assert access_level(user) == level, user Real output of the Python version ============================= test session starts ============================== collecting ... collected 5 items test_prog.py::test_admin_has_full_access[root-3] PASSED [ 20%] test_prog.py::test_admin_has_full_access[ops-3] PASSED [ 40%] test_prog.py::test_customer_access_level[asha-1] PASSED [ 60%] test_prog.py::test_customer_access_level[ravi-1] PASSED [ 80%] test_prog.py::test_customer_access_level[meena-2] PASSED [100%] ============================== 5 passed in 0.05s =============================== P1"],[1,"pairwise","Pairwise browser combinations","I solved it Generate test configurations for 3 browsers, 3 operating systems and 2 languages that cover every pair of values, using a greedy method, and feed them to a data provider. Example all combinations: 18, pairwise rows: 9 chrome windows en Pick, again and again, the full combination that covers the most uncovered pairs. Covering all pairs needs 9 runs instead of 18 here, and the saving grows quickly with more factors. Each row is still a real, complete configuration. ⏱️ Time O(runs × combinations)💾 Auxiliary space O(pairs) Solution: Pairwise browser combinations from itertools import product import pytest FACTORS = [[\"chrome\", \"firefox\", \"safari\"], [\"windows\", \"mac\", \"linux\"], [\"en\", \"hi\"]] def pairs(c): return {f\"0{c[0]}|1{c[1]}\", f\"0{c[0]}|2{c[2]}\", f\"1{c[1]}|2{c[2]}\"} def pairwise(): all_rows = list(product(*FACTORS)) uncovered = set().union(*(pairs(c) for c in all_rows)) chosen = [] while uncovered: best = max(all_rows, key=lambda c: len(pairs(c) & uncovered)) # max keeps the first best, like the Java loop chosen.append(best) uncovered -= pairs(best) return chosen ROWS = pairwise() print(f\"all combinations: {3 * 3 * 2}, pairwise rows: {len(ROWS)}\") @pytest.mark.parametrize(\"browser, os_name, lang\", ROWS) def test_checkout_works(browser, os_name, lang): print(f\" {browser:<8} {os_name:<8} {lang}\") Real output of the Python version ============================= test session starts ============================== collecting ... all combinations: 18, pairwise rows: 9 collected 9 items test_prog.py::test_checkout_works[chrome-windows-en] chrome windows en PASSED test_prog.py::test_checkout_works[chrome-mac-hi] chrome mac hi PASSED test_prog.py::test_checkout_works[firefox-windows-hi] firefox windows hi PASSED test_prog.py::test_checkout_works[firefox-mac-en] firefox mac en PASSED test_prog.py::test_checkout_works[safari-linux-en] safari linux en PASSED test_prog.py::test_checkout_works[chrome-linux-hi] chrome linux hi PASSED test_prog.py::test_checkout_works[safari-windows-hi] safari windows hi PASSED test_prog.py::test_checkout_works[firefox-linux-en] firefox linux en PASSED test_prog.py::test_checkout_works[safari-mac-en] safari mac en PASSED ============================== 9 passed in 0.05s =============================== P2"],[1,"thread-local","A ThreadLocal driver in parallel","I solved it Run four tests with parallel=\"methods\". Each test gets its own fake driver from a ThreadLocal set in @BeforeMethod. Prove that no driver is used by two threads. Example tests run: 4 drivers created: 4 each test had its own driver: true drivers shared between threads: 0 Call driver.remove() in @AfterMethod so a reused thread does not keep an old driver. ThreadLocal gives every thread a private slot. Shared result maps use ConcurrentHashMap and counters use AtomicInteger, so the bookkeeping itself is thread safe. ⏱️ Time O(tests)💾 Auxiliary space O(threads) Solution: A ThreadLocal driver in parallel # The same idea in Python: threading.local() gives each thread its own driver.import threading import time from concurrent.futures importThreadPoolExecutor local = threading.local() lock = threading.Lock() created, used_by, clashes = [0], {}, [0] classFakeDriver: def __init__(self): with lock: created[0] += 1 self.id = created[0] def run_test(name): local.driver = FakeDriver() # @BeforeMethod my_id = local.driver.id time.sleep(0.1) if local.driver.id != my_id: with lock: clashes[0] += 1with lock: used_by[name] = my_id del local.driver # @AfterMethoddef test_each_thread_gets_its_own_driver(): withThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=4) as pool: list(pool.map(run_test, [\"login\", \"search\", \"cart\", \"profile\"])) print(f\"\\ntests run: {len(used_by)}\") print(f\"drivers created: {created[0]}\") print(f\"each test had its own driver: {str(len(set(used_by.values())) == len(used_by)).lower()}\") print(f\"drivers shared between threads: {clashes[0]}\") assert clashes[0] == 0 Real output of the Python version ============================= test session starts ============================== collecting ... collected 1 item test_prog.py::test_each_thread_gets_its_own_driver tests run: 4 drivers created: 4 each test had its own driver: true drivers shared between threads: 0 PASSED ============================== 1 passed in 0.05s ==============================="],[1,"thread-local","Summary","The one idea to keep from each lesson. Open a lesson again when a line does not make sense yet. The testng.xml suite filetestng.xml describes a run without touching the test code: which classes, which methods, in which <test> blocks. One suite holds one or more tests, each test holds classes, and each class can include or exclude methods. Groups for smoke and regression runsA test can belong to several groups: @Test(groups = {\"smoke\", \"login\"}). The suite file then runs a slice of the project, such as every smoke test except the ones known to be broken. Exclude always wins over include. Parameters from testng.xml@Parameters({\"browser\", \"baseUrl\"}) fills method arguments from <parameter> tags. A parameter in a <test> overrides the same name at suite level, so one suite can run the same class on Chrome and Firefox. Data providersA data provider is a method that returns rows of arguments. TestNG calls the test once per row and reports each run with its arguments, so a failure names the exact data that broke it. Data providers that read filesA provider can return an Iterator<Object[]> instead of an array, so rows are produced one at a time. It can also take a Method argument to know which test is asking, and live in a separate class shared through dataProviderClass. Parallel execution"],[1,"comic","Decide what runs, where, with which data","Tap any panel to flip it for the deeper idea, or press Play the comic. 📌 Remember Keep the what, where and which data in configuration, and keep tests free of shared state."],[1,"real-life","Real life, just funnier","The same idea is hiding in things you use every day. 🎛️A music playlist The songs stay the same, the playlist decides which play tonight and in what order. testng.xml is the playlist for tests. 🏪A shop opening checklist The quick morning check (smoke) and the full weekly audit (regression) use items from the same list. Groups pick which items count today. 🧾A form filled for each customer The clerk follows the same steps for every customer in the queue. A data provider is the queue, the test is the clerk. Hi, I am MiraI will read \"Suites, Groups, Parameters and Data Providers\" to you 🐢🐇 Part 1 of 8Welcome Once a project has hundreds of tests, the questions change: which tests run tonight, on which browser, with which data, and how fast. TestNG answers them outside the test code, in testng xml, groups, parameters and data providers. Every Java example runs on real TestNG with its suite file shown beside it. Voice settings Voice In English, read the full notes of each part instead of the short summary Scroll the page along with me ⚠️ No English voice is installed on this device, so your browser may read in another accent or stay silent. Microsoft Edge offers free online English voices, or you can add one in your device's speech settings. The captions above are always there to read along."],[2,"","Listeners, Retries, Factories and Framework Design","The parts of TestNG that turn tests into a framework: ITestListener for screenshots and logs on failure, IRetryAnalyzer for flaky infrastructure, @Factory for running a class once per data set, SkipException for conditional skips, the Page Object Model with a BaseTest, reports and testng-failed.xml, and how it all fits into Maven and a CI pipeline. · ITestListener · listener · @Listeners · onTestFailure · screenshot on failure · ITestResult · ITestContext · ISuiteListener · IReporter · IRetryAnalyzer · retryAnalyzer · flaky test · @Factory · factory with data provider · SkipException · conditional skip · Page Object Model · page object · BaseTest · WebDriver · driver factory · locators · testng-failed.xml · emailable-report.html · test-output · Maven Surefire · CI pipeline · Jenkins · GitHub Actions · pytest hooks · conftest.py · pytest-rerunfailures · mocha retries ✅ Brainy Bytes · TestNG ✅ TestNG›🧪 TestNG Framework›Advanced TestNG 🛠️ Step 1 · Topic 3 of 3 · Beginner to advanced"],[2,"top","","A suite that runs every night needs more than assertions. It needs a screenshot when something fails, a second chance for a timeout on a slow server, the same checks for every language or role, and a clean structure so that one change in the login page touches one file. This page builds those pieces on real TestNG. ⏱️ About 8 min read📚 6 parts🎬 2 animations🏋️ 8 practice problems🎯 16 quiz questions🧮 8 calculationsJava with real TestNG, with pytest (Python) and Mocha (JavaScript) side by sideThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Listeners 2Retrying flaky tests 3@Factory: one class, many instances 4Conditional skips 5A framework: Page Objects and a BaseTest 6Reports, reruns and CI Lesson 1 of 6"],[2,"listeners","Listeners","A listener is a class TestNG calls on every test event: start, success, failure, skip, and the start and end of each <test>. Frameworks use it to take a screenshot when a test fails, write logs, or feed a report, without adding a single line to the tests themselves. 🎬 Animated diagram"],[2,"listeners","Where a listener hooks into a test run","Press Play, or step with the arrows, to follow the story one stage at a time. ↔ Swipe sideways to see the whole diagram This is the whole picture. Press ▶ Play to walk through it step by step. 0 / 4 Way to register Code On a class @Listeners(ScreenshotListener.class) For the whole suite <listeners><listener class-name=\"com.shop.ScreenshotListener\"/></listeners> in testng.xml Automatically A META-INF/services/org.testng.ITestNGListener file listing the class A listener that saves a screenshot on failure # pytest's hooks play the role of TestNG listeners. They normally live in conftest.py.# pytest_runtest_makereport sees each test's result, which is where screenshots are taken.import pytest passed, failed = [], [] @pytest.hookimpl(hookwrapper=True) def pytest_runtest_makereport(item, call): outcome = yield rep = outcome.get_result() if rep.when == \"call\": if rep.passed: passed.append(item.name) print(f\"\\n[listener] PASS {item.name}\") elif rep.failed: failed.append(item.name) print(f\"\\n[listener] FAIL {item.name}: {call.excinfo.value}\") print(f\"[listener] saved screenshots/{item.name}.png\") def pytest_sessionfinish(session): print(f\"\\n[listener] finished: {len(passed)} passed, {len(failed)} failed\") def test_open_cart(): passdef test_apply_coupon(): assert\"SAVE5\" == \"SAVE10\", \"coupon shown\" Real output of the Python version ============================= test session starts ============================== collecting ... collected 2 items test_prog.py::test_open_cart [listener] PASS test_open_cart PASSED test_prog.py::test_apply_coupon [listener] FAIL test_apply_coupon: coupon shown assert 'SAVE5' == 'SAVE10' - SAVE10 + SAVE5 [listener] saved screenshots/test_apply_coupon.png FAILED [listener] finished: 1 passed, 1 failed =================================== FAILURES =================================== ______________________________ test_apply_coupon _______________________________ def test_apply_coupon(): > assert \"SAVE5\" == \"SAVE10\", \"coupon shown\" E AssertionError: coupon shown E assert 'SAVE5' == 'SAVE10' E E - SAVE10 E + SAVE5 test_prog.py:31: AssertionError =========================== short test summary info ============================ FAILED test_prog.py::test_apply_coupon - AssertionError: coupon shown ========================= 1 failed, 1 passed in 0.05s ========================== In a real suite, onTestFailure gets the driver from the test instance (result.getInstance()) and calls ((TakesScreenshot) driver).getScreenshotAs(OutputType.FILE). In pytest the same job is done by the pytest_runtest_makereport hook in conftest.py, and in Mocha by an afterEach that checks this.currentTest.state. Lesson 2 of 6"],[2,"retry","Retrying flaky tests","An IRetryAnalyzer decides whether a failed test gets another attempt. It suits failures caused by the environment, such as a slow test server or a network blip. It never fixes a real bug: a wrong total fails on every attempt. A slow page passes on the third try, a real bug fails three times # pytest-rerunfailures adds @pytest.mark.flaky(reruns=2). This small decorator shows the same idea.import functools def retry(times): def wrap(fn): @functools.wraps(fn) def run(*a, **k): for attempt inrange(times + 1): try: return fn(*a, **k) exceptAssertionError: if attempt == times: raiseprint(f\" retry {attempt + 1} for {fn.__name__}\") return run return wrap attempts = {\"search\": 0, \"payment\": 0} @retry(2) def test_search_loads(): # fails twice (slow server), then passes attempts[\"search\"] += 1print(f\"searchLoads attempt {attempts['search']}\") assert attempts[\"search\"] >= 3, \"results did not load in time\"@retry(2) def test_payment_total(): # a real bug: retrying cannot fix it attempts[\"payment\"] += 1print(f\"paymentTotal attempt {attempts['payment']}\") assert99 + 1 == 101, \"total\" Real output of the Python version ============================= test session starts ============================== collecting ... collected 2 items test_prog.py::test_search_loads searchLoads attempt 1 retry 1 for test_search_loads searchLoads attempt 2 retry 2 for test_search_loads searchLoads attempt 3 PASSED test_prog.py::test_payment_total paymentTotal attempt 1 retry 1 for test_payment_total paymentTotal attempt 2 retry 2 for test_payment_total paymentTotal attempt 3 FAILED =================================== FAILURES =================================== ______________________________ test_payment_total ______________________________ @retry(2) def test_payment_total(): # a real bug: retrying cannot fix it attempts[\"payment\"] += 1 print(f\"paymentTotal attempt {attempts['payment']}\") > assert 99 + 1 == 101, \"total\" E AssertionError: total E assert (99 + 1) == 101 test_prog.py:30: AssertionError =========================== short test summary info ============================ FAILED test_prog.py::test_payment_total - AssertionError: total ========================= 1 failed, 1 passed in 0.05s ========================== Retries hide problems if nobody looks at them. Count and report retried tests, keep the limit low (one or two), and treat a test that needs retries every night as a defect in the test or the environment. Lesson 3 of 6"],[2,"factory","@Factory: one class, many instances","A data provider repeats one method with different arguments. A factory repeats a whole class: it returns several instances, each built with different constructor data, and every @Test of the class runs on every instance. That fits \"run this whole checklist for each language\" or \"for each user role\". Every test in the class runs for English, Hindi and German # A parametrised class runs every test once per parameter set, like TestNG's @Factory.import pytest def home_title(lang): return {\"en\": \"Welcome\", \"hi\": \"स्वागत है\"}.get(lang, \"Willkommen\") @pytest.mark.parametrize(\"lang, greeting\", [(\"en\", \"Welcome\"), (\"hi\", \"स्वागत है\"), (\"de\", \"Willkommen\")], ids=[\"en\", \"hi\", \"de\"]) classTestLanguage: def test_title_is_translated(self, lang, greeting): print(f\"{lang}: title {home_title(lang)}\") assert home_title(lang) == greeting def test_url_has_language(self, lang, greeting): print(f\"{lang}: url https://shop.example.com/{lang}/\") Real output of the Python version ============================= test session starts ============================== collecting ... collected 6 items test_prog.py::TestLanguage::test_title_is_translated[en] en: title Welcome PASSED test_prog.py::TestLanguage::test_title_is_translated[hi] hi: title स्वागत है PASSED test_prog.py::TestLanguage::test_title_is_translated[de] de: title Willkommen PASSED test_prog.py::TestLanguage::test_url_has_language[en] en: url https://shop.example.com/en/ PASSED test_prog.py::TestLanguage::test_url_has_language[hi] hi: url https://shop.example.com/hi/ PASSED test_prog.py::TestLanguage::test_url_has_language[de] de: url https://shop.example.com/de/ PASSED ============================== 6 passed in 0.05s =============================== @Factory(dataProvider = \"roles\") on a constructor combines both ideas: the provider supplies the rows and the factory builds one instance per row. Lesson 4 of 6"],[2,"skip","Conditional skips","Sometimes a test cannot run meaningfully: the payment sandbox is down, or a destructive test must never run outside the QA environment. Throwing SkipException marks the test as skipped with a reason, which is honest: it did not pass and it did not find a product bug. Skipping for a down service and for the wrong environment import pytest env = \"staging\"# would come from config payment_sandbox_up = False# would come from a health check callprint(f\"env = {env}, payment sandbox up = {payment_sandbox_up}\") def test_browse_catalogue(): print(\"browseCatalogue ran\") def test_pay_by_card(): ifnot payment_sandbox_up: pytest.skip(\"payment sandbox is down, not a product failure\") @pytest.mark.skipif(env != \"qa\", reason=f\"destructive test runs only on qa, this is {env}\") def test_delete_all_orders(): print(\"never printed\") Real output of the Python version ============================= test session starts ============================== collecting ... env = staging, payment sandbox up = False collected 3 items test_prog.py::test_browse_catalogue browseCatalogue ran PASSED test_prog.py::test_pay_by_card SKIPPED (payment sandbox is down, not...) test_prog.py::test_delete_all_orders SKIPPED (destructive test runs ...) =========================== short test summary info ============================ SKIPPED [1] test_prog.py:14: payment sandbox is down, not a product failure SKIPPED [1] test_prog.py:17: destructive test runs only on qa, this is staging ========================= 1 passed, 2 skipped in 0.05s ========================= Lesson 5 of 6"],[2,"framework","A framework: Page Objects and a BaseTest","In the Page Object Model, each page of the application gets a class that holds its locators and actions, such as loginAs(user, pass). Tests talk to pages, never to locators. When a button's id changes, one page class changes and every test keeps working. A BaseTest class creates and quits the driver, so test classes only extend it. 🎬 Animated diagram"],[2,"framework","Layers of a TestNG UI framework","Press Play, or step with the arrows, to follow the story one stage at a time. ↔ Swipe sideways to see the whole diagram This is the whole picture. Press ▶ Play to walk through it step by step. 0 / 4 LoginPage, BaseTest and two tests, with a fake driver import pytest classFakeDriver: # stands in for Selenium's WebDriverdef __init__(self): self.fields, self.page = {}, \"about:blank\"def get(self, url): self.page = url print(f\" driver.get({url})\") def type(self, locator, text): self.fields[locator] = text def click(self, locator): if locator == \"id=login\": self.page = \"/dashboard\"if self.fields.get(\"id=password\") == \"Admin@123\"else\"/login?error\"def text(self, locator): return\"Invalid credentials\"if self.page.endswith(\"error\") else f\"Welcome, {self.fields.get('id=user')}\"def quit(self): print(\" driver.quit()\") classLoginPage: # Page Object: locators and actions, no assertionsUSER, PASS, BUTTON, MESSAGE = \"id=user\", \"id=password\", \"id=login\", \"css=.msg\"def __init__(self, d): self.d = d def open(self): self.d.get(\"https://shop.example.com/login\"); return self def login_as(self, u, p): self.d.type(self.USER, u); self.d.type(self.PASS, p); self.d.click(self.BUTTON); return self def message(self): return self.d.text(self.MESSAGE) def current_page(self): return self.d.page @pytest.fixture def driver(): # the fixture is pytest's BaseTestprint(\"\\n @BeforeMethod: new driver\") d = FakeDriver() yield d d.quit() def test_valid_user_reaches_dashboard(driver): page = LoginPage(driver).open().login_as(\"asha\", \"Admin@123\") assert page.current_page() == \"/dashboard\"assert page.message() == \"Welcome, asha\"print(\" validUserReachesDashboard checked\") def test_wrong_password_shows_error(driver): page = LoginPage(driver).open().login_as(\"asha\", \"nope\") assert page.message() == \"Invalid credentials\"print(\" wrongPasswordShowsError checked\") Real output of the Python version ============================= test session starts ============================== collecting ... collected 2 items test_prog.py::test_valid_user_reaches_dashboard @BeforeMethod: new driver driver.get(https://shop.example.com/login) validUserReachesDashboard checked PASSED driver.quit() test_prog.py::test_wrong_password_shows_error @BeforeMethod: new driver driver.get(https://shop.example.com/login) wrongPasswordShowsError checked PASSED driver.quit() ============================== 2 passed in 0.05s =============================== src/test/java/com/shop/ base/BaseTest.java driver setup and teardown, ThreadLocal<WebDriver> pages/LoginPage.java locators and actions, no assertions pages/CartPage.java tests/LoginTests.java assertions only, extends BaseTest listeners/ScreenshotListener.java utils/ConfigReader.java reads config.properties src/test/resources/ config.properties testdata/login.csv testng.xml Keep assertions in tests and out of page objects. A page method returns what it sees, and the test decides whether that is right, so the same page can serve positive and negative tests. Lesson 6 of 6"],[2,"reports-ci","Reports, reruns and CI","File after a run Use emailable-report.html A single page summary to attach or email index.html The full browsable report testng-results.xml Machine readable results that CI servers and report tools import testng-failed.xml A ready suite with only the failed and skipped tests: run it to rerun the failures <!-- pom.xml, inside <build><plugins> --> <plugin> <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId> <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId> <version>3.2.5</version> <configuration> <suiteXmlFiles><suiteXmlFile>testng.xml</suiteXmlFile></suiteXmlFiles> </configuration> </plugin> mvn test runs the suite mvn test -Dbrowser=firefox passes a system property the ConfigReader can read A CI server such as Jenkins or GitHub Actions runs mvn test on every change or every night, publishes testng-results.xml as test results, and keeps the screenshots and HTML reports as build artifacts. Many teams add Extent Reports or Allure on top, both of which plug in as TestNG listeners. 🧮"],[2,"numbers","Number crunch","Work it out, type your answer and press Check. Stuck? Peek at the hint, then the full working. 0/8correct C1 A factory makes 4 instances of a class with 3 tests. How many test runs? Instances × tests Answer: 12 4 × 3 = 12 C2 A run has 3 passed, 1 failed and 1 skipped. What percent of executed tests passed? Skipped tests were not executed. Answer: 75 33 + 1 × 100 = 75% C1 With a retry limit of 2, what is the most attempts one failing test can make? First try plus retries. Answer: 3 1 + 2 = 3 C2 A page loads after 1.2 s. A wait polls every 0.5 s starting at 0. After how many seconds does it see the page? Polls at 0, 0.5, 1.0, 1.5 Answer: 1.5 The first poll at or after 1.2 s is at 1.5 s C1 A fixed 5 s sleep is used 40 times in a suite where the waits are really needed for 1.2 s each. How many seconds are wasted per run? Per sleep: 5 − 1.2 Answer: 152 40 × 3.8 = 152 seconds C2 In 200 nightly tests, 6 fail on the first try and 4 of those pass on retry. How many tests end up failed? Failures minus recovered retries. Answer: 2 6 − 4 = 2, and the 4 recovered ones should still be reported C1 Three roles each check 3 actions. One role has one wrong permission. How many individual permission checks fail? Count checks, not roles. Answer: 1 Of 3 × 3 = 9 checks, exactly 1 fails C2 A suite takes 40 minutes on 1 thread. With 4 threads and 10% overhead, about how many minutes? Divide, then add 10%. Answer: 11 404 × 1.1 = 11 minutes 🎯"],[2,"quiz","Quiz time","Four levels from easy to expert. Code in the questions is labelled with its language. Every answer comes with the reason behind it. 0/16correct Q1 Which listener method runs when a test fails? The name says it. onTestFailure(ITestResult) is the usual place for a screenshot. Q2 Which TestNG interface decides whether a failed test runs again? Retry. retry(ITestResult) returns true to run the test again. Q3 In the Page Object Model, where do locators live? One class per page. Page classes hold locators and actions, tests hold assertions. Q4 Which file lets you rerun only the tests that failed? It has failed in its name. TestNG writes it after every run with the failed and skipped tests. Q1 How do you register a listener for one class? An annotation on the class. Or list it under <listeners> in testng.xml for the whole suite. Q2 A test fails because 99 + 1 is compared with 101, and it has a retry limit of 2. How many attempts run? The first run plus two retries. It fails every time, so all 3 attempts run and the test is reported as failed. Q3 What does throwing SkipException in a test do? Honest reporting. The test shows as skipped, and the message explains why. Q4 Which statement fits page objects best? Separation of concerns. Keeping assertions in tests lets one page serve positive and negative tests. Q1 A @Factory returns 3 instances and the class has 2 @Test methods. How many test runs? Every test on every instance. 3 × 2 = 6. Q2 In pytest, where do listener style hooks such as pytest_runtest_makereport usually live? A shared configuration file. conftest.py hooks apply to every test in its folder and below. Q3 Why is a retry analyzer risky if nobody tracks retries? A pass after retries looks like a plain pass. Report retried tests, or real intermittent defects slip through as green builds. Q4 In Mocha, how do you allow a test two more attempts? Arrow functions have no this. Use a regular function so this refers to the Mocha context. Q1 A listener needs the WebDriver for a screenshot. Where can it get it? The result knows which object ran the test. Cast result.getInstance() to BaseTest and read its (ThreadLocal) driver. Q2 @Factory(dataProvider = \"roles\") with 3 rows on a class with 1 test. How many instances and runs? One instance per row. The provider gives 3 rows, the factory builds 3 instances, each runs the 1 test. Q3 Why prefer an explicit wait over Thread.sleep(5000) in UI tests? Think of a page that loads in 1.2 s. The wait finishes after about 1.2 s, the sleep wastes 3.8 s on every run and still fails if the page needs 6 s. Q4 Which file do CI servers usually import to show TestNG results? Machine readable. XML result files feed CI dashboards. HTML reports are for people. 🏋️"],[2,"quiz","Practice problems","Try each one yourself first. Every solution is written in Python, JavaScript and Java, was run for real, and can be replayed line by line. 0/8solved P1"],[2,"result-counter","A listener that sets the build colour","I solved it Write an ITestListener that counts passed, failed and skipped tests and, in onFinish, prints the pass rate of executed tests and GREEN or RED. Example passed 3, failed 1, skipped 1, pass rate 75.0% of executed build: RED Skipped tests did not execute, so leave them out of the rate. The listener sees every result without the tests knowing it exists. The same counts feed a Slack message or a dashboard in real projects. ⏱️ Time O(tests)💾 Auxiliary space O(1) Solution: A listener that sets the build colour import pytest counts = {\"passed\": 0, \"failed\": 0, \"skipped\": 0} def pytest_runtest_logreport(report): # a hook: called for every test phaseif report.when == \"call\"or (report.when == \"setup\"and report.skipped): counts[report.outcome] += 1def pytest_sessionfinish(session): run = counts[\"passed\"] + counts[\"failed\"] print(f\"\\npassed {counts['passed']}, failed {counts['failed']}, skipped {counts['skipped']}, \" f\"pass rate {100 * counts['passed'] / run:.1f}% of executed\") print(\"build: GREEN\"if counts[\"failed\"] == 0else\"build: RED\") def test_a(): passdef test_b(): passdef test_c(): pytest.fail(\"price mismatch\") def test_d(): pass@pytest.mark.skip(reason=\"depends on c\") def test_e(): pass Real output of the Python version ============================= test session starts ============================== collecting ... collected 5 items test_prog.py::test_a PASSED [ 20%] test_prog.py::test_b PASSED [ 40%] test_prog.py::test_c FAILED [ 60%] test_prog.py::test_d PASSED [ 80%] test_prog.py::test_e SKIPPED (depends on c) [100%] passed 3, failed 1, skipped 1, pass rate 75.0% of executed build: RED =================================== FAILURES =================================== ____________________________________ test_c ____________________________________ > def test_c(): pytest.fail(\"price mismatch\") ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ E Failed: price mismatch test_prog.py:20: Failed =========================== short test summary info ============================ FAILED test_prog.py::test_c - Failed: price mismatch ==================== 1 failed, 3 passed, 1 skipped in 0.05s ==================== P2"],[2,"search-page","A search page object","I solved it Write a SearchPage with searchFor, resultCount and titles, then five tests: matching, case, spaces, blank input and an unknown word. Example PASSED: findsAllPens PASSED: blankSearchShowsNothing Return this from searchFor so calls can be chained. Tests read like the requirement: page.searchFor(\"pen\").resultCount(). If the search box locator changes, only SearchPage changes. ⏱️ Time O(products)💾 Auxiliary space O(results) Solution: A search page object import pytest PRODUCTS = [\"Red Pen\", \"Blue Pen\", \"Notebook A5\", \"Pencil Box\", \"Pen Stand\"] classSearchPage: # Page Object over a fake shopdef __init__(self): self.query = \"\"def search_for(self, text): self.query = text return self def titles(self): q = self.query.strip().lower() return [p for p inPRODUCTSif q and q in p.lower()] def result_count(self): returnlen(self.titles()) @pytest.fixture def page(): returnSearchPage() def test_finds_all_pens(page): assert page.search_for(\"pen\").result_count() == 4def test_ignores_case(page): assert page.search_for(\"NOTEBOOK\").titles()[0] == \"Notebook A5\"def test_trims_spaces(page): assert page.search_for(\" box \").result_count() == 1def test_blank_search_shows_nothing(page): assert page.search_for(\" \").result_count() == 0def test_unknown_word_shows_nothing(page): assert page.search_for(\"laptop\").titles() == [] Real output of the Python version ============================= test session starts ============================== collecting ... collected 5 items test_prog.py::test_finds_all_pens PASSED [ 20%] test_prog.py::test_ignores_case PASSED [ 40%] test_prog.py::test_trims_spaces PASSED [ 60%] test_prog.py::test_blank_search_shows_nothing PASSED [ 80%] test_prog.py::test_unknown_word_shows_nothing PASSED [100%] ============================== 5 passed in 0.05s =============================== P1"],[2,"slowest-tests","Find the slowest tests","I solved it Write a listener that records each test's duration and prints them slowest first, flagging any over a 250 ms budget. Example slowest first: search about 300 ms <- over the 250 ms budget ITestResult has getStartMillis() and getEndMillis(). Rounding to 100 ms keeps the report stable between runs. Watching durations catches tests that slowly get worse long before they time out. ⏱️ Time O(n log n)💾 Auxiliary space O(n) Solution: Find the slowest tests import time took = {} def pytest_runtest_logreport(report): if report.when == \"call\": took[report.nodeid.split(\"::\")[-1]] = report.duration * 1000def pytest_sessionfinish(session): print(\"\\nslowest first:\") for name, ms in sorted(took.items(), key=lambda kv: -kv[1]): flag = \" <- over the 250 ms budget\"if ms > 250else\"\"print(f\" {name:<10} about {round(ms / 100) * 100} ms{flag}\") def test_login(): time.sleep(0.1) def test_search(): time.sleep(0.3) def test_checkout(): time.sleep(0.2) Real output of the Python version ============================= test session starts ============================== collecting ... collected 3 items test_prog.py::test_login PASSED [ 33%] test_prog.py::test_search PASSED [ 66%] test_prog.py::test_checkout PASSED [100%] slowest first: test_search about 300 ms <- over the 250 ms budget test_checkout about 200 ms test_login about 100 ms ============================== 3 passed in 0.05s =============================== P2"],[2,"role-factory","A factory fed by a data provider","I solved it Build one RoleTests instance per role (admin, editor, viewer) with @Factory(dataProvider = \"roles\") and check view, edit and delete against the rules. One permission is wrong in the app. Example editor view=true edit=true delete=true FAILED: permissionsMatchTheRules The factory constructor receives one row of the provider. The permission matrix is checked in full for every role. The failure names the role and action, here an editor who can delete. ⏱️ Time O(roles × actions)💾 Auxiliary space O(roles) Solution: A factory fed by a data provider import pytest RULES = {\"admin\": {\"view\", \"edit\", \"delete\"}, \"editor\": {\"view\", \"edit\"}, \"viewer\": {\"view\"}} def app(role, action): # the app under test, with one bugif role == \"editor\"and action == \"delete\": returnTrue# bug: editors can deletereturn action inRULES[role] @pytest.mark.parametrize(\"role\", [\"admin\", \"editor\", \"viewer\"]) classTestRole: def test_permissions_match_the_rules(self, role): print(f\"{role:<7}\" + \"\".join(f\" {a}={str(app(role, a)).lower():<5}\"for a in [\"view\", \"edit\", \"delete\"])) for a in [\"view\", \"edit\", \"delete\"]: assert app(role, a) == (a inRULES[role]), f\"{role} {a}\" Real output of the Python version ============================= test session starts ============================== collecting ... collected 3 items test_prog.py::TestRole::test_permissions_match_the_rules[admin] admin view=true edit=true delete=true PASSED test_prog.py::TestRole::test_permissions_match_the_rules[editor] editor view=true edit=true delete=true FAILED test_prog.py::TestRole::test_permissions_match_the_rules[viewer] viewer view=true edit=false delete=false PASSED =================================== FAILURES =================================== ______________ TestRole.test_permissions_match_the_rules[editor] _______________ self = <test_prog.TestRole object>, role = 'editor' def test_permissions_match_the_rules(self, role): print(f\"{role:<7}\" + \"\".join(f\" {a}={str(app(role, a)).lower():<5}\" for a in [\"view\", \"edit\", \"delete\"])) for a in [\"view\", \"edit\", \"delete\"]: > assert app(role, a) == (a in RULES[role]), f\"{role} {a}\" E AssertionError: editor delete E assert True == ('delete' in {'edit', 'view'}) E + where True = app('editor', 'delete') test_prog.py:17: AssertionError =========================== short test summary info ============================ FAILED test_prog.py::TestRole::test_permissions_match_the_rules[editor] - Ass... ========================= 1 failed, 2 passed in 0.05s ========================== P1"],[2,"rerun-file","Build a rerun file for failures","I solved it Write a listener that collects failed and skipped tests and prints a testng.xml that includes only them, the way TestNG writes testng-failed.xml. Example 3 method(s) to rerun: <include name=\"search\"/> <include name=\"sortByPrice\"/> Skipped tests belong in the rerun too, because they never got a fair run. Rerunning only failures shortens the feedback loop after a fix. pytest offers the same through --last-failed, Mocha through --grep. ⏱️ Time O(tests)💾 Auxiliary space O(failures) Solution: Build a rerun file for failures # pytest keeps failures in its cache, so \"pytest --last-failed\" reruns them.# This hook writes the list the same way TestNG writes testng-failed.xml.import pytest failed = [] def pytest_runtest_logreport(report): if (report.when == \"call\"and report.failed) or report.skipped: failed.append(report.nodeid.split(\"::\")[-1]) def pytest_sessionfinish(session): print(f\"\\n{len(failed)} method(s) to rerun:\") print(\"pytest \" + \" \".join(f\"test_prog.py::{m}\"for m in failed)) def test_login(): passdef test_search(): pytest.fail(\"no results\") def test_filter(): pass@pytest.mark.skip(reason=\"depends on search\") def test_sort_by_price(): passdef test_logout(): pytest.fail(\"menu hidden\") Real output of the Python version ============================= test session starts ============================== collecting ... collected 5 items test_prog.py::test_login PASSED [ 20%] test_prog.py::test_search FAILED [ 40%] test_prog.py::test_filter PASSED [ 60%] test_prog.py::test_sort_by_price SKIPPED (depends on search) [ 80%] test_prog.py::test_logout FAILED [100%] 3 method(s) to rerun: pytest test_prog.py::test_search test_prog.py::test_sort_by_price test_prog.py::test_logout =================================== FAILURES =================================== _________________________________ test_search __________________________________ > def test_search(): pytest.fail(\"no results\") ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ E Failed: no results test_prog.py:19: Failed _________________________________ test_logout __________________________________ > def test_logout(): pytest.fail(\"menu hidden\") ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ E Failed: menu hidden test_prog.py:23: Failed =========================== short test summary info ============================ FAILED test_prog.py::test_search - Failed: no results FAILED test_prog.py::test_logout - Failed: menu hidden ==================== 2 failed, 2 passed, 1 skipped in 0.05s ==================== P2"],[2,"explicit-wait","An explicit wait with a fake clock","I solved it Write Wait.until(condition, timeout, poll) using a fake clock. Show it succeeding for a spinner that disappears at 1.2 s and timing out for a toast that never appears. Example spinner: found=true after 1500 ms and 4 polls toast: found=false after 2000 ms and 5 polls Check the condition, then sleep one poll interval, until the next poll would pass the deadline. This is how WebDriverWait works. A fake clock makes the test instant and repeatable, a useful trick for any time based code. ⏱️ Time O(timeout ÷ poll)💾 Auxiliary space O(1) Solution: An explicit wait with a fake clock classFakeClock: def __init__(self): self.now = 0def sleep(self, ms): self.now += ms def wait_until(clock, condition, timeout_ms, poll_ms): \"\"\"The idea behind Selenium's WebDriverWait(driver, 5).until(...).\"\"\" polls, end = 0, clock.now + timeout_ms whileTrue: polls += 1if condition(): returnTrue, polls if clock.now + poll_ms > end: returnFalse, polls clock.sleep(poll_ms) def test_spinner_disappears_in_time(): clock = FakeClock() ok, polls = wait_until(clock, lambda: clock.now >= 1200, 5000, 500) print(f\"\\nspinner: found={str(ok).lower()} after {clock.now} ms and {polls} polls\") assert ok def test_toast_never_shows_and_the_wait_times_out(): clock = FakeClock() ok, polls = wait_until(clock, lambda: False, 2000, 500) print(f\"\\ntoast: found={str(ok).lower()} after {clock.now} ms and {polls} polls\") assertnot ok def test_fixed_sleep_wastes_time(): print(f\"\\nfixed sleep wastes {5000 - 1200} ms on every run\") Real output of the Python version ============================= test session starts ============================== collecting ... collected 3 items test_prog.py::test_spinner_disappears_in_time spinner: found=true after 1500 ms and 4 polls PASSED test_prog.py::test_toast_never_shows_and_the_wait_times_out toast: found=false after 2000 ms and 5 polls PASSED test_prog.py::test_fixed_sleep_wastes_time fixed sleep wastes 3800 ms on every run PASSED ============================== 3 passed in 0.05s =============================== P1"],[2,"product-page","Check a product page with soft asserts","I solved it A ProductPage returns title, price, stock, rating and delivery. Check all five against the catalogue with SoftAssert, parsing the price from \"₹ 799\". Example checked 5 fields in one pass price after the sale expected [749.0] but found [799.0], rating shown 4.3 should be at least 4.5 Strip everything except digits and the dot before parsing the price. The page object hides how values are read, and the test lists every mismatch at once, so one run gives the developer the whole list. ⏱️ Time O(fields)💾 Auxiliary space O(failures) Solution: Check a product page with soft asserts import re SHOWN = {\"title\": \"Wireless Mouse M200\", \"price\": \"₹ 799\", \"stock\": \"In stock\", \"rating\": \"4.3\", \"delivery\": \"Tomorrow\"} def price(): return float(re.sub(r\"[^0-9.]\", \"\", SHOWN[\"price\"])) def test_details_match_the_catalogue(): errors = [] def check(ok, msg): ifnot ok: errors.append(msg) check(SHOWN[\"title\"] == \"Wireless Mouse M200\", \"title\") check(price() == 749.0, f\"price after the sale expected [749.0] but found [{price()}]\") check(SHOWN[\"stock\"] == \"In stock\", \"stock\") check(float(SHOWN[\"rating\"]) >= 4.5, f\"rating shown {SHOWN['rating']} should be at least 4.5\") check(SHOWN[\"delivery\"] == \"Tomorrow\", \"delivery\") print(\"\\nchecked 5 fields in one pass\") assertnot errors, \"The following asserts failed:\\n\" + \",\\n\".join(errors) Real output of the Python version ============================= test session starts ============================== collecting ... collected 1 item test_prog.py::test_details_match_the_catalogue checked 5 fields in one pass FAILED =================================== FAILURES =================================== _______________________ test_details_match_the_catalogue _______________________ def test_details_match_the_catalogue(): errors = [] def check(ok, msg): if not ok: errors.append(msg) check(SHOWN[\"title\"] == \"Wireless Mouse M200\", \"title\") check(price() == 749.0, f\"price after the sale expected [749.0] but found [{price()}]\") check(SHOWN[\"stock\"] == \"In stock\", \"stock\") check(float(SHOWN[\"rating\"]) >= 4.5, f\"rating shown {SHOWN['rating']} should be at least 4.5\") check(SHOWN[\"delivery\"] == \"Tomorrow\", \"delivery\") print(\"\\nchecked 5 fields in one pass\") > assert not errors, \"The following asserts failed:\\n\" + \",\\n\".join(errors) E AssertionError: The following asserts failed: E price after the sale expected [749.0] but found [799.0], E rating shown 4.3 should be at least 4.5 E assert not ['price after the sale expected [749.0] but found [799.0]', 'rating shown 4.3 should be at least 4.5'] test_prog.py:21: AssertionError =========================== short test summary info ============================ FAILED test_prog.py::test_details_match_the_catalogue - AssertionError: The f... ============================== 1 failed in 0.05s =============================== P2"],[2,"retry-config","A retry limit from configuration","I solved it Read the retry limit from -Dretries (default 1). Three tests pass on attempt 1, 2 and 3. Report how many times each ran. Example retries allowed: 1 needsThreeTries ran 2 time(s) passesOnSecondTry ran 2 time(s) A new analyzer instance is created per test method, so the counter inside it is per test. With one retry, the test that needs three attempts still fails, which is the honest outcome. CI can pass a different limit without code changes. ⏱️ Time O(attempts)💾 Auxiliary space O(tests) Solution: A retry limit from configuration import functools import os MAX = int(os.environ.get(\"RETRIES\", \"1\")) # like -Dretries=N attempts = {} def retry(fn): @functools.wraps(fn) def run(): for i inrange(MAX + 1): try: return fn() exceptAssertionError: if i == MAX: raisereturn run def flaky(name, pass_on): attempts[name] = attempts.get(name, 0) + 1return attempts[name] >= pass_on @retrydef test_passes_first_time(): assert flaky(\"passesFirstTime\", 1) @retrydef test_passes_on_second_try(): assert flaky(\"passesOnSecondTry\", 2) @retrydef test_needs_three_tries(): assert flaky(\"needsThreeTries\", 3) def test_zz_report(): print(f\"\\nretries allowed: {MAX}\") for k in sorted(attempts): print(f\" {k} ran {attempts[k]} time(s)\") Real output of the Python version ============================= test session starts ============================== collecting ... collected 4 items test_prog.py::test_passes_first_time PASSED test_prog.py::test_passes_on_second_try PASSED test_prog.py::test_needs_three_tries FAILED test_prog.py::test_zz_report retries allowed: 1 needsThreeTries ran 2 time(s) passesFirstTime ran 1 time(s) passesOnSecondTry ran 2 time(s) PASSED =================================== FAILURES =================================== ____________________________ test_needs_three_tries ____________________________ @retry > def test_needs_three_tries(): assert flaky(\"needsThreeTries\", 3) ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ E AssertionError: assert False E + where False = flaky('needsThreeTries', 3) test_prog.py:32: AssertionError =========================== short test summary info ============================ FAILED test_prog.py::test_needs_three_tries - AssertionError: assert False ========================= 1 failed, 3 passed in 0.05s =========================="],[2,"retry-config","Summary","The one idea to keep from each lesson. Open a lesson again when a line does not make sense yet. ListenersA listener is a class TestNG calls on every test event: start, success, failure, skip, and the start and end of each <test>. Retrying flaky testsAn IRetryAnalyzer decides whether a failed test gets another attempt. It suits failures caused by the environment, such as a slow test server or a network blip. It never fixes a real bug: a wrong total fails on every attempt. @Factory: one class, many instancesA data provider repeats one method with different arguments. A factory repeats a whole class: it returns several instances, each built with different constructor data, and every @Test of the class runs on every instance. Conditional skipsSometimes a test cannot run meaningfully: the payment sandbox is down, or a destructive test must never run outside the QA environment. A framework: Page Objects and a BaseTestIn the Page Object Model, each page of the application gets a class that holds its locators and actions, such as loginAs(user, pass). Tests talk to pages, never to locators. When a button's id changes, one page class changes and every test keeps working. Reports, reruns and CIA CI server such as Jenkins or GitHub Actions runs mvn test on every change or every night, publishes testng-results.xml as test results, and keeps the screenshots and HTML reports as build artifacts."],[2,"comic","From tests to a framework","Tap any panel to flip it for the deeper idea, or press Play the comic. 📌 Remember Let listeners watch, keep retries rare and visible, and keep locators in page classes."],[2,"real-life","Real life, just funnier","The same idea is hiding in things you use every day. 🎥A dashcam It records all the time, and nobody looks at it until something goes wrong. A failure listener with screenshots is the dashcam of a test suite. 📞Redialling a dropped call If the line drops, you call again. If the number is wrong, redialling a hundred times will not help. That is the difference between a flaky test and a real bug. 🏢A building directory Visitors read the directory instead of memorising every office. Tests use page objects instead of memorising locators, so moving an office means updating one board. Hi, I am MiraI will read \"Listeners, Retries, Factories and Framework Design\" to you 🐢🐇 Part 1 of 8Welcome A suite that runs every night needs more than assertions: a screenshot when something fails, a second chance for a timeout on a slow server, the same checks for every language or role, and a structure where one change in the login page touches one file. This page builds those pieces on real TestNG. Voice settings Voice In English, read the full notes of each part instead of the short summary Scroll the page along with me ⚠️ No English voice is installed on this device, so your browser may read in another accent or stay silent. Microsoft Edge offers free online English voices, or you can add one in your device's speech settings. The captions above are always there to read along."],[3,"","Cheatsheet","The key idea of every lesson, short code examples, tables, tips, common mistakes and exam notes from the TestNG chapters on one page, from @Test and the annotation lifecycle to testng.xml suites, data providers, listeners, retries and parallel runs. Search it, filter it, print it or download the PDF. · cheatsheet · cheat sheet · formula sheet · revision · PDF · download · quick reference · @Test · annotations · testng.xml · DataProvider · listeners · retry · parallel · soft assert · groups · dependsOnMethods"],[3,"step-1","Step 1TestNG Framework","1.1"],[3,"testng-foundations","TestNG Foundations: Annotations, Assertions and Test Control","Open chapter A main method can run checks, but it cannot report which ones passed, skip the rest when setup fails, or rerun only the failures. TestNG does all of that through annotations. Every example on this page runs on real TestNG, and the same idea is shown in pytest and Mocha, so you can compare the three most common test runners for testers. Key ideas Why a test framework Test classes go in src/test/java . Run them from the IDE (right click, Run), from a testng.xml file, or with mvn test , where the Maven Surefire plugin finds the tests. A first test class A test method is a public void method marked @Test . It calls the code under test and checks the result with an assertion . If every assertion holds, the test passes. The annotation lifecycle Hard and soft assertions Test attributes Without priorities, TestNG runs test methods of a class in alphabetical order , not in the order they are written. Tests should not depend on that order. When order matters, say so with priority or dependsOnMethods . Dependencies between tests dependsOnMethods makes a test wait for others and run only if they passed. If a dependency fails, the dependent test is skipped , not failed, so the report shows one real failure instead of a cascade of misleading ones. Tables Table: need · plain main method · testng Need Plain main method TestNG Know which checks passed Read the printed lines yourself Pass, fail and skip counts, HTML and XML reports Keep going after one failure The first exception stops everything Each @Test is independent Set up and clean up Copy the code into every check @BeforeMethod, @AfterClass and the rest Choose what to run Comment code in and out testng.xml, groups, include and exclude Run with different data Loops written by hand @DataProvider and @Parameters Rerun only failures Not possible testng-failed.xml, retry analyzers Lesson: Why a test framework Table: annotation · runs · typical use in ui automation Annotation Runs Typical use in UI automation @BeforeSuite / @AfterSuite Once for the whole run Start and close the report, load configuration @BeforeTest / @AfterTest Once per <test> tag in testng.xml Set the browser for that block of tests @BeforeClass / @AfterClass Once per test class Launch and quit the browser @BeforeMethod / @AfterMethod Before and after every @Test Open the start page, clear cookies, take a screenshot on failure Lesson: The annotation lifecycle Table: attribute · effect Attribute Effect priority = 1 Lower numbers run first. The default is 0, and negative values are allowed enabled = false The test is not run at all and not counted description = \"…\" Text shown in the reports timeOut = 200 Fails the test if it takes longer than 200 milliseconds invocationCount = 3 Runs the same test 3 times (add threadPoolSize to run them in parallel) expectedExceptions = X.class The test passes only if X is thrown. With expectedExceptionsMessageRegExp, the message must match too Lesson: Test attributes Tips and traps Remember The examples here run on TestNG 6.9, which is what this site's code runner has. Everything shown works the same in TestNG 7, the version to use in new projects. In pytest the same roles are played by test functions and fixtures, in Mocha by describe, it and hooks.Lesson: Why a test framework Remember TestNG's Assert.assertEquals(actual, expected) takes the actual value first. JUnit uses the opposite order. Swapping them still passes and fails correctly, but the failure message then says \"expected 3 but found 4\", which sends you looking in the wrong place.Lesson: A first test class Remember pytest builds the same layers with fixture scopes: session, module, class and function. The code before yield is the setup, the code after it the teardown. Mocha has before, beforeEach, afterEach and after.Lesson: The annotation lifecycle Common mistake Forgetting soft.assertAll() is a classic bug: every check fails silently and the test still passes. Create a new SoftAssert inside each test, never share"],[3,"testng-foundations","TestNG Foundations: Annotations, Assertions and Test Control","one between tests.Lesson: Hard and soft assertions In the exam Prefer independent tests. Each one should prepare its own state, for example by logging in through an API in @BeforeMethod. Dependencies make sense for real end to end flows, where a later step truly cannot start without the earlier one.Lesson: Dependencies between tests 1.2"],[3,"testng-suites-data","Suites, Groups, Parameters and Data Providers","Open chapter Once a project has hundreds of tests, the questions change: which tests run tonight, on which browser, with which data, and how fast. TestNG answers them outside the test code, in testng.xml, groups, parameters and data providers. Every Java example on this page runs on real TestNG with the suite file shown beside it. Key ideas The testng.xml suite file testng.xml describes a run without touching the test code: which classes, which methods, in which <test> blocks. One suite holds one or more tests , each test holds classes, and each class can include or exclude methods. Groups for smoke and regression runs A test can belong to several groups : @Test(groups = {\"smoke\", \"login\"}) . The suite file then runs a slice of the project, such as every smoke test except the ones known to be broken. Exclude always wins over include. Parameters from testng.xml @Parameters({\"browser\", \"baseUrl\"}) fills method arguments from <parameter> tags. A parameter in a <test> overrides the same name at suite level, so one suite can run the same class on Chrome and Firefox. Data providers A data provider is a method that returns rows of arguments. TestNG calls the test once per row and reports each run with its arguments, so a failure names the exact data that broke it. Data providers that read files A provider can return an Iterator<Object[]> instead of an array, so rows are produced one at a time. It can also take a Method argument to know which test is asking, and live in a separate class shared through dataProviderClass . Parallel execution Tables Table: parallel= · what runs at the same time parallel= What runs at the same time \"methods\" Test methods, even from the same class \"classes\" Whole classes, methods inside a class stay sequential \"tests\" Each <test> block, for example one per browser \"instances\" Instances of the same class, useful with @Factory Lesson: Parallel execution Tips and traps Remember Classes are named with their package, for example com.shop.tests.LoginTests. <packages><package name=\"com.shop.tests\"/></packages> runs every class in a package. With Maven, point the Surefire plugin at the file with <suiteXmlFile>testng.xml</suiteXmlFile>.Lesson: The testng.xml suite file Common mistake Configuration methods such as @BeforeMethod and @BeforeGroups belong to no group by default, so a group filter leaves them out. Add alwaysRun = true to setup methods, or give them the same groups, or your tests will run without their setup.Lesson: Groups for smoke and regression runs Remember pytest's @pytest.mark.parametrize is the same idea, and each row appears in the report as test_login[admin-wrong-invalid]. Mocha has no built in equivalent, so tests loop over the rows and create one it() per row.Lesson: Data providers Common mistake Parallel tests must not share changeable state. A plain int counter or a single WebDriver field shared by threads gives random failures. Use AtomicInteger and concurrent collections for counters, and ThreadLocal<WebDriver> so each thread drives its own browser.Lesson: Parallel execution 1.3"],[3,"testng-advanced","Listeners, Retries, Factories and Framework Design","Open chapter A suite that runs every night needs more than assertions. It needs a screenshot when something fails, a second chance for a timeout on a slow server, the same checks for every language or role, and a clean structure so that one change in the login page touches one file. This page builds those pieces on real TestNG. Key ideas Listeners A listener is a class TestNG calls on every test event: start, success, failure, skip, and the start and end of each <test> . Retrying flaky tests An IRetryAnalyzer decides whether a failed test gets another attempt. It suits failures caused by the environment, such as a slow test server or a network blip. It never fixes a real bug: a wrong total fails on every attempt. @Factory: one class, many instances A data provider repeats one method with different arguments. A factory repeats a whole class: it returns several instances, each built with different constructor data, and every @Test of the class runs on every instance. Conditional skips Sometimes a test cannot run meaningfully: the payment sandbox is down, or a destructive test must never run outside the QA environment. A framework: Page Objects and a BaseTest In the Page Object Model , each page of the application gets a class that holds its locators and actions, such as loginAs(user, pass) . Tests talk to pages, never to locators. When a button's id changes, one page class changes and every test keeps working. Reports, reruns and CI A CI server such as Jenkins or GitHub Actions runs mvn test on every change or every night, publishes testng-results.xml as test results, and keeps the screenshots and HTML reports as build artifacts. Tables Table: way to register · code Way to register Code On a class @Listeners(ScreenshotListener.class) For the whole suite <listeners><listener class-name=\"com.shop.ScreenshotListener\"/></listeners> in testng.xml Automatically A META-INF/services/org.testng.ITestNGListener file listing the class Lesson: Listeners Table: file after a run · use File after a run Use emailable-report.html A single page summary to attach or email index.html The full browsable report testng-results.xml Machine readable results that CI servers and report tools import testng-failed.xml A ready suite with only the failed and skipped tests: run it to rerun the failures Lesson: Reports, reruns and CI Tips and traps Remember In a real suite, onTestFailure gets the driver from the test instance (result.getInstance()) and calls ((TakesScreenshot) driver).getScreenshotAs(OutputType.FILE). In pytest the same job is done by the pytest_runtest_makereport hook in conftest.py, and in Mocha by an afterEach that checks this.currentTest.state.Lesson: Listeners Common mistake Retries hide problems if nobody looks at them. Count and report retried tests, keep the limit low (one or two), and treat a test that needs retries every night as a defect in the test or the environment.Lesson: Retrying flaky tests Remember @Factory(dataProvider = \"roles\") on a constructor combines both ideas: the provider supplies the rows and the factory builds one instance per row.Lesson: @Factory: one class, many instances In the exam Keep assertions in tests and out of page objects. A page method returns what it sees, and the test decides whether that is right, so the same page can serve positive and negative tests.Lesson: A framework: Page Objects and a BaseTest"],[4,"","Interview Questions","TestNG questions from automation interviews: annotation order, testng.xml, groups, dependencies, data providers, soft asserts, listeners, retries, parallel runs and Maven. · interview · interview questions · viva · FAQ · job preparation · annotation order · testng.xml · groups · dependsOnMethods · DataProvider · SoftAssert · ITestListener · IRetryAnalyzer · parallel · surefire TestNG · Interview Questions 🎤"],[4,"","TestNG Interview Questions","The TestNG questions asked in automation tester interviews: annotations and their order, testng.xml, groups, dependencies, data providers, listeners, retries, parallel runs and how TestNG fits into a Selenium framework with Maven and CI. ✍️ Read the question and write your own answer in the box, as you would say it in the interview. 💡 Stuck? Open the hint. It points you in the right direction without giving the answer away. ✅ Open the answer to compare. You get the model answer, key points and a detailed explanation. 🎯 Rate yourself. Your notes and ratings stay in this browser, so you can filter to the ones to review. 🎤"],[4,"interview","TestNG Interview Questions","Answer as if the interviewer asked \"where did you use this in your framework\". Open the hint only if you are stuck. 0/29known ⭐ Most important topics for interviews 🏷️ Annotations and order The order of @BeforeSuite to @AfterSuite is asked in almost every automation interview. 📄 testng.xml Suites, tests, classes, include and exclude, parameters and parallel settings. 🧺 Groups and dependencies Smoke and regression groups, dependsOnMethods and alwaysRun. 🗃️ DataProvider Data driven tests from arrays, Excel, JSON and parallel data providers. ✔️ Assertions Hard versus soft assertions and assertAll. 👂 Listeners and retry ITestListener for screenshots and reports, IRetryAnalyzer for flaky tests. ⚡ Parallel execution parallel and thread-count, and keeping WebDriver thread safe with ThreadLocal. 🛠️ Maven and CI Running suites with Surefire, passing parameters and reading reports in CI. 0 of 29 answers opened, 0 known, 0 to review Basics Q1 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptBasics What is TestNG and why would you choose it over JUnit 4? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first NG stands for Next Generation. Think of what it added for large test suites. Your answer Model answer TestNG is a Java testing framework inspired by JUnit and NUnit. Compared with JUnit 4 it adds suite level setup, groups, test dependencies, data providers, parameters from XML, built in parallel execution, listeners and HTML reports, which make it popular for Selenium and API automation suites. Detailed explanation JUnit 5 has closed much of the gap with tags, parameterised tests and extensions. TestNG is still common in automation because of testng.xml suites, dependsOnMethods and simple parallel settings. Follow up questions the interviewer may ask Have you used JUnit 5? What would you miss from TestNG? How did you do? Annotations Q2 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptAnnotations What is the execution order of TestNG annotations? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Go from the biggest container to the smallest, then back out. Your answer Model answer @BeforeSuite, @BeforeTest, @BeforeClass, @BeforeMethod, @Test, @AfterMethod, @AfterClass, @AfterTest, @AfterSuite. @BeforeGroups and @AfterGroups run before the first and after the last test method of a group. Suite, Test, Class, Method, then the test After annotations run in the reverse order @BeforeMethod runs before every @Test Detailed explanation Suite means the whole <suite> in testng.xml, Test means one <test> tag inside it, which may contain many classes, Class means each test class, and Method wraps each @Test method, including each row from a data provider. A typical framework opens the report in @BeforeSuite, reads config in @BeforeTest, starts the browser in @BeforeMethod and quits it in @AfterMethod. How did you do? Q3 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptAnnotations Name the most useful attributes of the @Test annotation. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Think of order, skipping, grouping, data, timing and expected failures. Your answer Model answer priority, enabled, groups, dependsOnMethods, dependsOnGroups, dataProvider, timeOut, invocationCount, threadPoolSize, expectedExceptions, alwaysRun, description and retryAnalyzer. Detailed explanation Examples: @Test(enabled = false) skips a test that is known to be broken, @Test(timeOut = 5000) fails a test that runs longer than 5 seconds, and @Test(expectedExceptions = IllegalArgumentException.class) passes only if that exception is thrown. How did you do? Q4 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptAnnotations How can you skip a test in TestNG? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first There is an attribute, an exception and an XML option. Your answer Model answer Set @Test(enabled = false), throw new SkipException(\"reason\") inside the test (useful for conditional skips such as \"feature not on this environment\"), or exclude the method or its group in testng.xml. Detailed explanation A test is also reported as skipped when a method it depends on fails, or when a @BeforeMethod fails, since the setup it needed did not complete. How did you"],[4,"interview","TestNG Interview Questions","do? Q5 🌿 Medium🖨️ OutputAnnotations What does this class print when it is run on its own? publicclassOrderTest { @BeforeClasspublicvoid bc() { System.out.println(\"BC\"); } @BeforeMethodpublicvoid bm() { System.out.println(\"BM\"); } @Testpublicvoid b() { System.out.println(\"b\"); } @Testpublicvoid a() { System.out.println(\"a\"); } @AfterMethodpublicvoid am() { System.out.println(\"AM\"); } @AfterClasspublicvoid ac() { System.out.println(\"AC\"); } } ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first BeforeMethod and AfterMethod wrap each test. Tests run in alphabetical order by default. Your answer Model answer It prints BC, BM, a, AM, BM, b, AM, AC. Without priorities TestNG runs test methods in alphabetical order of their names, and the method level setup and teardown run around each test. Detailed explanation Do not rely on alphabetical order in real suites. Use priority or dependsOnMethods when order matters, and better still keep tests independent. BCBM a AMBM b AMAC How did you do? Q6 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptAnnotations How does priority work? What happens with negative values, equal values and tests without a priority? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Lower numbers run first, and the default value is 0. Your answer Model answer Tests run from the lowest priority number to the highest. Negative values are allowed and run before 0. A test without a priority has the default of 0. Tests with the same priority run in alphabetical order of their method names. Detailed explanation Priority only orders tests, it does not create a dependency. If a high priority login test fails, a later test still runs. Use dependsOnMethods if a test should be skipped when another fails. How did you do? Q7 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptAnnotations What is the difference between dependsOnMethods and priority? What does alwaysRun do? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first One of them skips tests when an earlier one fails. Your answer Model answer dependsOnMethods makes a test wait for other methods and skips it if any of them failed. priority only sets the order. alwaysRun = true on a dependent test makes it run even if the methods it depends on failed. On a configuration method such as @AfterMethod it makes the method run regardless of groups and failures. Detailed explanation A soft dependency, written with alwaysRun = true, keeps the order but not the skipping. Use alwaysRun = true on teardown so the browser is closed even when a test or setup failed or when you run only some groups. How did you do? testng.xml Q8 🌱 Easy📘 Concepttestng.xml What is testng.xml and what can you configure in it? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Suites, tests, classes, groups, parameters, listeners, parallel. Your answer Model answer testng.xml defines what to run and how. In it you set the suite, one or more tests, the classes, packages or methods inside each test, groups to include or exclude, parameters, listeners, and parallel settings such as parallel and thread-count. Detailed explanation Keeping several XML files, such as smoke.xml and regression.xml, lets CI choose a suite without code changes. <suite name=\"Regression\" parallel=\"classes\" thread-count=\"4\"> <listeners> <listener class-name=\"com.shop.listeners.TestListener\"/> </listeners> <test name=\"Chrome\"> <parameter name=\"browser\" value=\"chrome\"/> <groups><run><include name=\"smoke\"/></run></groups> <classes> <class name=\"com.shop.tests.LoginTest\"/> <class name=\"com.shop.tests.CartTest\"/> </classes> </test> </suite> How did you do? Q9 🌿 Medium📘 Concepttestng.xml How do you pass values such as browser and URL from testng.xml to a test? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first An annotation reads a <parameter> tag. Your answer Model answer Declare <parameter name=\"browser\" value=\"chrome\"/> in the suite or test tag, and read it with @Parameters(\"browser\") on a test or configuration method that takes a matching argument. @Optional(\"chrome\") gives a default when the XML does not set it. Detailed explanation A parameter at <test> level overrides one with"],[4,"interview","TestNG Interview Questions","the same name at <suite> level. For values that change per CI run, many teams read System.getProperty(\"browser\") instead, passed with -Dbrowser=firefox on the Maven command line. @Parameters({\"browser\", \"baseUrl\"}) @BeforeMethodpublicvoid setUp(@Optional(\"chrome\") String browser, String baseUrl) { DriverManager.start(browser); DriverManager.get().get(baseUrl); } How did you do? Q10 🌿 Medium📘 Concepttestng.xml How do groups work, and how do you run only smoke tests? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Tag the methods, then include or exclude in XML. Your answer Model answer Tag tests with @Test(groups = {\"smoke\", \"regression\"}). In testng.xml use <groups><run><include name=\"smoke\"/></run></groups>. Exclude wins over include, so a test in both an included and an excluded group does not run. Detailed explanation Configuration methods such as @BeforeMethod do not run for a group selection unless they belong to that group or have alwaysRun = true. This is the most common reason the browser does not open when running only smoke tests. Groups can also be chosen from Maven with -Dgroups=smoke. How did you do? Q11 🌿 Medium📘 Concepttestng.xml What is the difference between @BeforeTest and @BeforeMethod? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first One of them matches a tag in testng.xml. Your answer Model answer @BeforeTest runs once before all classes inside one <test> tag of testng.xml. @BeforeMethod runs before every single @Test method. Detailed explanation People often confuse \"Test\" in @BeforeTest with a test method. If a <test> tag contains three classes with ten methods, @BeforeTest runs once and @BeforeMethod runs ten times. How did you do? Data driven testing Q12 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptData driven testing What is a @DataProvider and how do you use it? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first A method that returns rows of arguments. Your answer Model answer A @DataProvider method returns Object[][] or Iterator<Object[]>, where each row is one set of arguments. A test with @Test(dataProvider = \"name\") runs once per row, and each run is reported separately. Detailed explanation The provider can live in another class if you add dataProviderClass = LoginData.class and make the provider method static. Returning an Iterator loads rows lazily, which helps with large files. @DataProvider(name = \"logins\") publicObject[][] logins() { returnnewObject[][] { {\"standard_user\", \"secret\", true}, {\"locked_user\", \"secret\", false}, }; } @Test(dataProvider = \"logins\") publicvoid login(String user, String pass, boolean ok) { assertEquals(newLoginPage().login(user, pass).isLoggedIn(), ok); } How did you do? Q13 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptData driven testing What is the difference between @Parameters and @DataProvider? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first How many times does the test run with each one? Your answer Model answer @Parameters passes single values from testng.xml, so the test runs once with them. It suits environment settings such as the browser. @DataProvider supplies many rows from Java code, so the test runs once per row. It suits test data such as many logins. Detailed explanation A data provider can be fed from Excel with Apache POI, CSV, JSON or a database, which is how most data driven frameworks are built. How did you do? Q14 🔥 Hard📘 ConceptData driven testing How do you run data provider rows in parallel? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first There is an attribute on the provider and a setting in testng.xml. Your answer Model answer Set @DataProvider(parallel = true). TestNG then runs the rows on a separate pool whose size is data-provider-thread-count in the suite tag, which is 10 by default. Detailed explanation Each row then runs on its own thread, so the driver must be in a ThreadLocal and test data must not be shared and changed between rows. How did you do? Q15 🔥 Hard🌍 ScenarioData driven testing Your manager wants test data in Excel. How would you connect it to TestNG? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Apache POI reads the sheet, and a data provider"],[4,"interview","TestNG Interview Questions","hands the rows to the test. Your answer Model answer Write a utility that opens the workbook with Apache POI, reads the sheet row by row with DataFormatter so numbers and dates come back as text, and returns an Object[][]. Call it from a @DataProvider, and point the test at that provider. Detailed explanation Good practices: keep the file path in config, close the workbook with try with resources, skip the header row, and give each row a test case id so the report shows which row failed. For large data, return an Iterator<Object[]>. How did you do? Assertions Q16 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptAssertions What is the difference between hard and soft assertions? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first One stops the test at the first failure. Your answer Model answer A hard assertion from Assert throws at the first failure and stops the test. A SoftAssert records failures and lets the test continue, and only softAssert.assertAll() at the end reports them and fails the test. Detailed explanation Forgetting assertAll() means the test passes even with failed checks, which is a common bug. Create a new SoftAssert inside each test method rather than sharing one as a field, or failures from one test leak into the next. SoftAssert soft = newSoftAssert(); soft.assertEquals(page.title(), \"Cart\"); soft.assertTrue(page.hasCheckoutButton(), \"checkout button\"); soft.assertAll(); How did you do? Q17 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptAssertions Which TestNG assertion methods do you use most? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Equality, truth, null checks and failing on purpose. Your answer Model answer assertEquals(actual, expected, message), assertNotEquals, assertTrue, assertFalse, assertNull, assertNotNull, assertSame and fail(message). Since TestNG 7 there is also assertThrows and expectThrows. Detailed explanation In TestNG the order is actual first, then expected, which is the reverse of JUnit. Getting it backwards makes failure messages confusing. Always add a message so the report explains what was being checked. How did you do? Listeners and reporting Q18 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptListeners and reporting What reports does TestNG create, and which reporting tools have you used with it? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Look in the test-output folder, then think of nicer third party reports. Your answer Model answer TestNG writes index.html, emailable-report.html and testng-results.xml in test-output (or under target/surefire-reports with Maven). Teams usually add Extent Reports or Allure for richer reports with steps, screenshots and history. Detailed explanation CI servers such as Jenkins read testng-results.xml or the JUnit style XML to show trends and failed tests. How did you do? Q19 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptListeners and reporting What are TestNG listeners? Which ones have you used? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Interfaces whose methods TestNG calls at events such as test failure. Your answer Model answer Listeners are interfaces that TestNG calls back at events. ITestListener has onTestStart, onTestSuccess, onTestFailure and onTestSkipped, and is used for screenshots and reports. ISuiteListener runs at suite start and end, IRetryAnalyzer retries failures, IAnnotationTransformer changes annotations at run time, IReporter builds custom reports, and IInvokedMethodListener runs around every method. Detailed explanation Register listeners in testng.xml with <listeners>, with @Listeners on a class, or through the service loader file. The XML way applies to the whole suite and keeps tests clean. How did you do? Q20 🌿 Medium🌍 ScenarioListeners and reporting How do you take a screenshot automatically when a test fails? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Use a listener method that is called on failure. Your answer Model answer Implement ITestListener.onTestFailure(ITestResult result), get the driver for the current thread, cast it to TakesScreenshot, save the file named after result.getName() and a time stamp, and attach it to the report. Detailed explanation With parallel runs, get"],[4,"interview","TestNG Interview Questions","the driver from your ThreadLocal manager, not from a static field. With Extent or Allure, attach the screenshot as Base64 so the report is a single file you can share. publicclassTestListenerimplementsITestListener { @Overridepublicvoid onTestFailure(ITestResult result) { File src = ((TakesScreenshot) DriverManager.get()).getScreenshotAs(OutputType.FILE); Path to = Path.of(\"screenshots\", result.getName() + \"_\" + System.currentTimeMillis() + \".png\"); try { Files.createDirectories(to.getParent()); Files.copy(src.toPath(), to); } catch (IOException e) { thrownewUncheckedIOException(e); } } } How did you do? Q21 🔥 Hard📘 ConceptListeners and reporting How do you rerun failed tests in TestNG? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first There is a per test way and a whole suite way. Your answer Model answer Implement IRetryAnalyzer, whose retry(ITestResult) returns true while a retry count is below the limit, and attach it with @Test(retryAnalyzer = Retry.class). To apply it to every test, set it in an IAnnotationTransformer. After a run TestNG also writes testng-failed.xml in the output folder, which you can run to repeat only the failures. Detailed explanation Retries hide flakiness, so log every retry and fix the cause, such as missing waits, shared data or test order dependencies. Keep the limit small, one or two tries. publicclassRetryimplementsIRetryAnalyzer { privateint count = 0; privatestaticfinalintMAX = 2; @Overridepublicboolean retry(ITestResult result) { return count++ < MAX; } } How did you do? Parallel execution Q22 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptParallel execution How do you run tests in parallel, and what values can parallel take? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Set it on the suite tag together with a thread count. Your answer Model answer Set parallel and thread-count on the <suite> tag. parallel can be methods, classes, tests or instances. thread-count is the size of the pool. Detailed explanation Value What runs at the same time tests Each <test> tag on its own thread, handy for one browser per test tag classes Each class on its own thread, methods inside a class run one after another methods Every test method on any free thread, the most parallel and the most likely to expose shared state instances Methods of the same instance on the same thread How did you do? Q23 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptParallel execution What do invocationCount and threadPoolSize do? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Run the same test many times. Your answer Model answer invocationCount = 5 runs the same test method five times. threadPoolSize = 3 runs those invocations on three threads at the same time. This is useful for a quick load check or for finding a flaky test. Detailed explanation It does not replace real performance tools such as JMeter or k6, but it is a cheap way to reproduce race conditions. How did you do? Q24 🔥 Hard🌍 ScenarioParallel execution After switching on parallel=\"methods\", tests start failing with stale sessions and wrong pages. What would you check? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Anything static or shared between tests is now shared between threads. Your answer Model answer Check for shared state. The usual cause is a static WebDriver, so move it to a ThreadLocal. Also check page objects stored as fields of the test class, shared test data such as one user account used by every test, a SoftAssert shared as a field, and report or logger objects that are not thread safe. Detailed explanation Each test should create its own data, such as a unique email, and clean up after itself. If tests in one class depend on each other, run that class with parallel=\"classes\" instead, or remove the dependency. How did you do? Advanced Q25 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptAdvanced How do you test that a method throws an exception? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first There is an attribute and there is an assertion. Your answer Model answer Use @Test(expectedExceptions = InvalidCouponException.class, expectedExceptionsMessageRegExp = \".*expired.*\"), or call"],[4,"interview","TestNG Interview Questions","Assert.assertThrows(InvalidCouponException.class, () -> cart.apply(\"OLD10\")) inside the test. Detailed explanation assertThrows is more precise, because the attribute passes if any line in the test throws that exception, even a setup line you did not mean to test. How did you do? Q26 🔥 Hard📘 ConceptAdvanced What is @Factory and how is it different from @DataProvider? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first One creates test class instances, the other passes arguments to a method. Your answer Model answer @Factory returns an array of test class instances, so every @Test method of the class runs once for each instance with that instance's fields. @DataProvider runs one test method many times with different arguments. Detailed explanation Use a factory when a whole class should run with different setups, for example the same checkout tests for each country or each user role. A factory can itself use a data provider to build its instances. How did you do? Q27 🔥 Hard📘 ConceptAdvanced What happens to the tests when a @BeforeMethod or @BeforeClass fails? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first The tests do not fail, but they do not pass either. Your answer Model answer The tests that needed that setup are skipped, not failed. If @BeforeClass fails, every test in the class is skipped. If @BeforeSuite fails, everything in the suite is skipped. By default, after one configuration failure TestNG also skips later configuration methods of the same kind. Detailed explanation Set configfailurepolicy=\"continue\" on the suite tag to keep trying configuration methods for later tests. Watch the skipped count in reports, since a large number of skips usually means a setup problem, not passing tests. How did you do? Maven and CI Q28 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptMaven and CI How do you run TestNG suites from Maven and in CI? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first The Surefire plugin can point at testng.xml files. Your answer Model answer Add the maven-surefire-plugin to pom.xml with <suiteXmlFiles> pointing at the XML file, then run mvn test. Make the file a property so CI can pick the suite, for example mvn test -DsuiteXmlFile=smoke.xml -Dbrowser=chrome. Detailed explanation In Jenkins or GitHub Actions, run the Maven command, publish the TestNG or Surefire XML results, and archive the HTML report and screenshots. Run headless browsers in CI and set a sensible thread-count for the agent size. <plugin> <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId> <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId> <configuration> <suiteXmlFiles> <suiteXmlFile>${suiteXmlFile}</suiteXmlFile> </suiteXmlFiles> </configuration> </plugin> How did you do? Q29 🧠 Expert🏗️ DesignMaven and CI Explain how TestNG fits into your Selenium framework, from the command line to the report. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Walk the path: Maven, testng.xml, listeners, base test, page objects, assertions, report. Your answer Model answer Maven Surefire starts the suite named on the command line. testng.xml chooses the groups, parameters, listeners and parallel mode. A BaseTest uses @BeforeMethod to create a thread safe driver from the browser parameter and @AfterMethod(alwaysRun = true) to quit it. Tests call page objects and use data providers for data. Listeners log each step, take screenshots on failure and retry known flaky tests. The report and screenshots are archived in CI. Detailed explanation Mention the reasons: XML suites let CI choose smoke or regression without code changes, ThreadLocal keeps parallel runs safe, alwaysRun teardown prevents browsers being left open, and listeners keep reporting code out of the tests. Follow up questions the interviewer may ask How long does your regression suite take and how did you reduce it? How do you handle test data cleanup? How did you do? No questions match these filters."]]};
